{:edb/db-before-t 2196 :edb/db-after-t 2197 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535509 50 #inst "2026-10-14T05:20:12.884Z" 13194139535509 17592186045417 true] [17592186054800 1038 #edb/ref 17592186510438 13194139535509 17592186045417 true] [17592186054807 1038 #edb/ref 17592186510413 13194139535509 17592186045417 true] [17592186061710 1075 #edb/ref 17592186509877 13194139535509 17592186045417 true] [17592186061710 1078 #edb/ref 17592186509877 13194139535509 17592186045417 true] [17592186061711 1075 #edb/ref 17592186509844 13194139535509 17592186045417 true] [17592186061711 1078 #edb/ref 17592186509844 13194139535509 17592186045417 true] [17592186061712 1075 #edb/ref 17592186509962 13194139535509 17592186045417 true] [17592186061712 1078 #edb/ref 17592186509962 13194139535509 17592186045417 true] [17592186063973 1071 "Find the values of $x$ that satisfy $2x - 5 \\leq 1$ and $7x +11 < -3$." 13194139535509 17592186045417 false] [17592186063973 1071 "Find the values of $x$ that satisfy $2x - 5 \\leq 1$ and $7x +11 < -3.$" 13194139535509 17592186045417 true] [17592186063973 1074 "To solve compound \"and\" inequalities, we solve each inequality separately and then find their intersection.\n\n- We start by solving $2x - 5 \\leq 1$:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}2x - 5 & \\le 1 \\\\ 2x - 5 + 5 & \\le 1 + 5 \\\\ 2x & \\le 6 \\\\ \\frac{2x}{2} & \\le \\frac{6}{2} \\\\ x & \\le 3\\end{bmatrix}$\n\n![](images/60/600e2c9a8560e61697b480de35a3d077d13624ba616514a060c4bcf4fa3f47e4.png)\n\n- Then, we solve $7x +11 < -3$:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}7x + 11 & < - 3 \\\\ 7x + 11 - 11 & < - 3 - 11 \\\\ 7x & < - 14 \\\\ \\frac{7x}{7} & < \\frac{-14}{7} \\\\ x & < - 2\\end{bmatrix}$\n\n![](images/b6/b6014786331de7693c3a1e0d502ea79fb049252fe9f3ecfcd49a873d996896fe.png)\n\nTo find the values of $x$ that satisfy both inequalities, we find where the two solutions overlap:\n\n![](images/2e/2e5425630ece3ab7006378a7930aae0452c3d5261e7bfb579c97daf572b71d22.png)\n\nThus, the final solution is $x < -2$." 13194139535509 17592186045417 false] [17592186063973 1074 "To solve compound \"and\" inequalities, we solve each inequality separately and then find their intersection. \n\n- We start by solving $2x - 5 \\leq 1$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-5 & \\le 1 \\\\ 2x-5+5 & \\le 1+5 \\\\ 2x & \\le 6 \\\\ \\frac{2x}{2} & \\le \\frac{6}{2} \\\\ x & \\le 3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](images/60/600e2c9a8560e61697b480de35a3d077d13624ba616514a060c4bcf4fa3f47e4.png)\n\n- Then, we solve $7x +11 < -3$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7x+11 & <-3 \\\\ 7x+11-11 & <-3-11 \\\\ 7x & <-14 \\\\ \\frac{7x}{7} & <\\frac{-14}{7} \\\\ x & <-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](images/b6/b6014786331de7693c3a1e0d502ea79fb049252fe9f3ecfcd49a873d996896fe.png)\n\nTo find the values of $x$ that satisfy both inequalities, we find where the two solutions overlap:\n\n![](images/2e/2e5425630ece3ab7006378a7930aae0452c3d5261e7bfb579c97daf572b71d22.png)\n\nThus, the final solution is $x < -2.$" 13194139535509 17592186045417 true] [17592186063975 1071 "What is the solution to the following system of inequalities?\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}5x-35\\ge 0 \\\\ 7x>21\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535509 17592186045417 true] [17592186063975 1071 "What is the solution to the following system of inequalities?\n${5x - 35 \\ge 0; 7x > 21$" 13194139535509 17592186045417 false] [17592186063975 1074 "To solve the system, we need to find the values of $x$ that satisfy both\n\n$$\n5x-35 \\geq 0\n$$\n\nand $7x > 21$ simultaneously.\n\n- First, we solve $5x-35 \\geq 0$:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}5x - 35 & \\ge 0 \\\\ 5x - 35 + 35 & \\ge 0 + 35 \\\\ 5x & \\ge 35 \\\\ \\frac{5x}{5} & \\ge \\frac{35}{5} \\\\ x & \\ge 7\\end{bmatrix}$\n\n![](images/48/4875553156dc3e4ec15ecdb6c848fd43d519941123d0d9763d209e9f1d6a52f6.png)\n\n- Then, we solve $7x > 21$:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}7x & > 21 \\\\ \\frac{7x}{7} & > \\frac{21}{7} \\\\ x & > 3\\end{bmatrix}$\n\n![](images/f7/f7a68da2d31f0bfe038252ac0dc9151d81bf854a7d33da591cbaef05f52bda28.png)\n\nTo find the values of $x$ that satisfy both inequalities, we find where the two solutions overlap:\n\n![](images/27/27fbd7222a4a02d4a14c17c4043b55a2828e66c2d4dcb019598ea5efef2089e6.png)\n\nThus, the final solution is\n\n$$\nx \\geq 7\n$$" 13194139535509 17592186045417 false] [17592186063975 1074 "To solve the system, we need to find the values of $x$ that satisfy both $5x-35 \\geq 0$ and $7x > 21$ simultaneously.\n\n- First, we solve $5x-35 \\geq 0\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x-35 & \\ge 0 \\\\ 5x-35+35 & \\ge 0+35 \\\\ 5x & \\ge 35 \\\\ \\frac{5x}{5} & \\ge \\frac{35}{5} \\\\ x & \\ge 7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](images/48/4875553156dc3e4ec15ecdb6c848fd43d519941123d0d9763d209e9f1d6a52f6.png)\n\n- Then, we solve $7x > 21\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7x & >21 \\\\ \\frac{7x}{7} & >\\frac{21}{7} \\\\ x & >3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](images/f7/f7a68da2d31f0bfe038252ac0dc9151d81bf854a7d33da591cbaef05f52bda28.png)\n\nTo find the values of $x$ that satisfy both inequalities, we find where the two solutions overlap:\n\n![](images/27/27fbd7222a4a02d4a14c17c4043b55a2828e66c2d4dcb019598ea5efef2089e6.png)\n\nThus, the final solution is $x \\geq 7.$" 13194139535509 17592186045417 true] [17592186063978 1071 "What is the solution to the following system of inequalities?\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2-7x\\ge 2 \\\\ 10x-3\\ge 7\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535509 17592186045417 true] [17592186063978 1071 "What is the solution to the following system of inequalities?\n${2 - 7x \\ge 2; 10x - 3 \\ge 7$" 13194139535509 17592186045417 false] [17592186063978 1074 "To solve the system, we need to find the values of $x$ that satisfy both\n\n$$\n2-7x \\geq 2\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n10x-3 \\geq 7\n$$\n\nsimultaneously.\n\n- First, we solve $2-7x \\geq 2$:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}2 - 7x & \\ge 2 \\\\ 2 - 7x - 2 & \\ge 2 - 2 \\\\ -7x & \\ge 0 \\\\ (-1) \\cdot (-7x) & \\le (-1) \\cdot 0 \\\\ 7x & \\le 0 \\\\ \\frac{7x}{7} & \\le \\frac{0}{7} \\\\ x & \\le 0\\end{bmatrix}$\n\n![](images/96/96957d6e6c105fd3734dbe478850287c1c8771873b94fedc4ee0ed5cebf41c40.png)\n\n- Then, we solve $10x-3 \\geq 7$:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}10x - 3 & \\ge 7 \\\\ 10x - 3 + 3 & \\ge 7 + 3 \\\\ 10x & \\ge 10 \\\\ \\frac{10x}{10} & \\ge \\frac{10}{10} \\\\ x & \\ge 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\n![](images/9d/9d2d2e7b05bd4bcaf964353266e35bb12f406372ade7255accf6dc3503046f5c.png)\n\nTo find the values of $x$ that satisfy both inequalities, we find where the two solutions overlap:\n\n![](images/98/98654d9ff727e680d0e1ba1ff88b9202248aba6a75a5002a01cd9d628cb6c5b3.png)\n\nIt turns out that the solutions do not overlap at all. Therefore, no values of $x$ satisfy both inequalities simultaneously. So, there is no solution." 13194139535509 17592186045417 false] [17592186063978 1074 "To solve the system, we need to find the values of $x$ that satisfy both $2-7x \\geq 2$ and $10x-3 \\geq 7$ simultaneously. \n\n- First, we solve $2-7x \\geq 2\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2-7x & \\ge 2 \\\\ 2-7x-2 & \\ge 2-2 \\\\ -7x & \\ge 0 \\\\ (-1)\\cdot (-7x) & \\le (-1)\\cdot 0 \\\\ 7x & \\le 0 \\\\ \\frac{7x}{7} & \\le \\frac{0}{7} \\\\ x & \\le 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](images/96/96957d6e6c105fd3734dbe478850287c1c8771873b94fedc4ee0ed5cebf41c40.png)\n\n- Then, we solve $10x-3 \\geq 7\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10x-3 & \\ge 7 \\\\ 10x-3+3 & \\ge 7+3 \\\\ 10x & \\ge 10 \\\\ \\frac{10x}{10} & \\ge \\frac{10}{10} \\\\ x & \\ge 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](images/9d/9d2d2e7b05bd4bcaf964353266e35bb12f406372ade7255accf6dc3503046f5c.png)\n\nTo find the values of $x$ that satisfy both inequalities, we find where the two solutions overlap:\n\n![](images/98/98654d9ff727e680d0e1ba1ff88b9202248aba6a75a5002a01cd9d628cb6c5b3.png)\n\nIt turns out that the solutions do not overlap at all. Therefore, no values of $x$ satisfy both inequalities simultaneously. So, there is no solution." 13194139535509 17592186045417 true] [17592186063979 1071 "Find the set of values of $x$ that satisfy $1 \\lt 3x-2 \\leq 7$ and $2-3x \\leq -4$." 13194139535509 17592186045417 false] [17592186063979 1071 "Find the set of values of $x$ that satisfy $1 \\lt 3x-2 \\leq 7$ and $2-3x \\leq -4.$" 13194139535509 17592186045417 true] [17592186063979 1074 "To solve compound \"and\" inequalities, we solve each inequality separately and then find their intersection.\n\n- First, we solve $1 \\lt 3x-2 \\leq 7 \\,$:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & < 3x - 2 \\le 7 \\\\ 1 + 2 & < 3x - 2 + 2 \\le 7 + 2 \\\\ 3 & < 3x \\le 9 \\\\ \\frac{3}{3} & < \\frac{3x}{3} \\le \\frac{9}{3} \\\\ 1 & < x \\le 3\\end{bmatrix}$\n\n![](images/34/341633f5e132e1ab07af8969161c20443d65aec7e89ce0bc600868504dd19f9b.png)\n\n- Then, we solve $2-3x \\leq -4 \\,$:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}2 - 3x & \\le - 4 \\\\ 2 - 3x - 2 & \\le - 4 - 2 \\\\ -3x & \\le - 6 \\\\ (-1) \\cdot (-3x) & \\ge (-1) \\cdot (-6) \\\\ 3x & \\ge 6 \\\\ \\frac{3x}{3} & \\ge \\frac{6}{3} \\\\ x & \\ge 2\\end{bmatrix}$\n\n![](images/06/06dafae801f6ed3900474c72437eb5a40376ed06bebf3c6eb568e6237c2d930d.png)\n\nTo find the values of $x$ that satisfy both inequalities, we find where the two solutions overlap:\n\n![](images/9b/9b3bd118d0940d6336a32c25b8556dccb3c4085f75da7d3d8628725cf9105071.png)\n\nThus, the final solution is $x \\in [2,3]$." 13194139535509 17592186045417 false] [17592186063979 1074 "To solve compound \"and\" inequalities, we solve each inequality separately and then find their intersection.\n\n- First, we solve $1 \\lt 3x-2 \\leq 7 \\,\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & <3x-2\\le 7 \\\\ 1+2 & <3x-2+2\\le 7+2 \\\\ 3 & <3x\\le 9 \\\\ \\frac{3}{3} & <\\frac{3x}{3}\\le \\frac{9}{3} \\\\ 1 & <x\\le 3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](images/34/341633f5e132e1ab07af8969161c20443d65aec7e89ce0bc600868504dd19f9b.png)\n\n- Then, we solve  $2-3x \\leq -4 \\,\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2-3x & \\le -4 \\\\ 2-3x-2 & \\le -4-2 \\\\ -3x & \\le -6 \\\\ (-1)\\cdot (-3x) & \\ge (-1)\\cdot (-6) \\\\ 3x & \\ge 6 \\\\ \\frac{3x}{3} & \\ge \\frac{6}{3} \\\\ x & \\ge 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](images/06/06dafae801f6ed3900474c72437eb5a40376ed06bebf3c6eb568e6237c2d930d.png)\n\nTo find the values of $x$ that satisfy both inequalities, we find where the two solutions overlap:\n\n![](images/9b/9b3bd118d0940d6336a32c25b8556dccb3c4085f75da7d3d8628725cf9105071.png)\n\n   \n\nThus, the final solution is $x \\in [2,3].$" 13194139535509 17592186045417 true] [17592186064237 1038 #edb/ref 17592186510367 13194139535509 17592186045417 true] [17592186064237 1038 #edb/ref 17592186510458 13194139535509 17592186045417 true] [17592186064238 1038 #edb/ref 17592186510423 13194139535509 17592186045417 true] [17592186064238 1038 #edb/ref 17592186510436 13194139535509 17592186045417 true] [17592186064239 1038 #edb/ref 17592186510442 13194139535509 17592186045417 true] [17592186064239 1038 #edb/ref 17592186510467 13194139535509 17592186045417 true] [17592186064240 1038 #edb/ref 17592186510429 13194139535509 17592186045417 true] [17592186064440 1050 780.0 13194139535509 17592186045417 true] [17592186064605 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535509 17592186045417 true] [17592186064605 1071 "Find the set of values of $x$ that satisfy $-1 \\le 2 - x \\le 3$ and $2x + 1 \\ge 5$. {{field-1}}\n$\\le x$\n{{field-2}} {{field-3}}" 13194139535509 17592186045417 false] [17592186064605 1071 "Find the set of values of $x$ that satisfy $-1\\le 2-x\\le 3$ and $2x+1\\ge 5.$\n\n{{field-1}}\n$\\le x$\n{{field-2}}{{field-3}}" 13194139535509 17592186045417 true] [17592186064605 1074 "To solve compound \"and\" inequalities, we solve each inequality separately and then find their intersection.\n\n- First, we solve $-1\\le 2-x\\le 3\\,:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & \\le 2-x\\le 3 \\\\ -1-2 & \\le 2-x-2\\le 3-2 \\\\ -3 & \\le -x\\le 1 \\\\ (-1)\\cdot (-3) & \\ge (-1)\\cdot (-x)\\ge (-1)\\cdot 1 \\\\ 3 & \\ge x\\ge -1 \\\\ -1 & \\le x\\le 3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](images/8f/8fe1b22970eac80550ae9cc8d2da4b34df6a853c717a7dd402b164dfd99356ce.png)\n\n- Then, we solve $2x+1\\ge 5\\,:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+1 & \\ge 5 \\\\ 2x+1-1 & \\ge 5-1 \\\\ 2x & \\ge 4 \\\\ \\frac{2x}{2} & \\ge \\frac{4}{2} \\\\ x & \\ge 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](images/eb/ebcff9656b5f2a1fd80624639cb91baa0a28b696aa261b555cf449f1c62894d1.png)\n\nTo find the values of $x$ that satisfy both inequalities, we find where the two solutions overlap:\n\n![](images/77/7731bef503cf45a465945f81e0e2512f65c3e109a38d6d06afe74e17269f8c36.png)\n\nThus, the final solution is $2\\le x\\le 3.$" 13194139535509 17592186045417 true] [17592186065437 1064 "Suppose we want to find all of the values of $x$ that satisfy *both*\n\n$$\n3x+9 \\geq 0\n$$\n\n*and*\n\n$$\n2x \\leq -2\n$$\n\nsimultaneously.\n\nTo do this, we solve each inequality individually and then compute the intersection of the two solutions.\n\n- We start by solving $3x + 9 \\geq 0$:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}3x + 9 & \\ge 0 \\\\ 3x + 9 - 9 & \\ge 0 - 9 \\\\ 3x & \\ge - 9 \\\\ \\frac{3x}{3} & \\ge \\frac{-9}{3} \\\\ x & \\ge - 3\\end{bmatrix}$\nThe solution to this inequality is shown on the number line below.![](images/51/5127629c09f32bb8ed7872b476322d6973e44e82be7977492c8dd0967d3cb0ec.png)\n\n- Then, we solve $2x \\leq -2$:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}2x & \\le - 2 \\\\ \\frac{2x}{2} & \\le \\frac{-2}{2} \\\\ x & \\le - 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nThe solution to this inequality is shown on the number line below.\n\n![](images/35/35e30bc9c45b08793d847c8750698f7d9dab781438405239ed4483ce82f2dcfb.png)\n\nTo find the values of $x$ that satisfy both inequalities, we find where the two solutions overlap:\n\n![](images/fe/fe4c223767cb06d9d620d32f5e668c7305688b3870bf0317304c56b11053e19f.png)\n\nThus, the final solution is\n\n$$\n\\color{blue}-3 \\leq x \\leq -1\n$$" 13194139535509 17592186045417 false] [17592186065437 1064 "Suppose we want to find all of the values of $x$ that satisfy *both* $3x+9 \\geq 0$ *and* $2x \\leq -2$ simultaneously. \n\nTo do this, we solve each inequality individually and then compute the intersection of the two solutions.\n\n- We start by solving $3x + 9 \\geq 0\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x+9 & \\ge 0 \\\\ 3x+9-9 & \\ge 0-9 \\\\ 3x & \\ge -9 \\\\ \\frac{3x}{3} & \\ge \\frac{-9}{3} \\\\ x & \\ge -3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution to this inequality is shown on the number line below.\n\n![](images/51/5127629c09f32bb8ed7872b476322d6973e44e82be7977492c8dd0967d3cb0ec.png)\n\n- Then, we solve $2x \\leq -2\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x & \\le -2 \\\\ \\frac{2x}{2} & \\le \\frac{-2}{2} \\\\ x & \\le -1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution to this inequality is shown on the number line below.\n\n![](images/35/35e30bc9c45b08793d847c8750698f7d9dab781438405239ed4483ce82f2dcfb.png)\n\nTo find the values of $x$ that satisfy both inequalities, we find where the two solutions overlap:\n\n![](images/fe/fe4c223767cb06d9d620d32f5e668c7305688b3870bf0317304c56b11053e19f.png)\n\nThus, the final solution is $\\color{blue}-3 \\leq x \\leq -1.$" 13194139535509 17592186045417 true] [17592186068428 1074 "As $x$ approaches $-1$, the value of $f(x)$ approaches $2$. Consequently,\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\,-1}f(x)=2\\,\n$$" 13194139535509 17592186045417 false] [17592186068428 1074 "As $x$ approaches $-1,$ the value of $f(x)$ approaches $2.$ Consequently, \n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\,-1}f(x)=2\\,.\n$$" 13194139535509 17592186045417 true] [17592186068533 1074 "As $x$ approaches $0$, the value of $f(x)$ approaches $1$. Consequently,\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\,0}f(x)= 1\\,\n$$\n\nRemember, it does not matter that the actual value of $f(0)$ is $2$. All that matters is that as $x$ approaches $0$, the function $f(x)$ *looks like* it is getting closer and closer to $1$." 13194139535509 17592186045417 false] [17592186068533 1074 "As $x$ approaches $0,$ the value of $f(x)$ approaches $1.$ Consequently, \n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\,0}f(x)= 1\\,.\n$$\n\nRemember, it does not matter that the actual value of $f(0)$ is $2.$ All that matters is that as $x$ approaches $0,$ the function $f(x)$ *looks like* it is getting closer and closer to $1$." 13194139535509 17592186045417 true] [17592186068534 1071 "Which of the following statements are true concerning the function $y=f(x)$ whose graph is shown below?\n\n1. $\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\,0}f(x)=0$\n2. $\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\,3}f(x)=0$\n3. $\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\,-1}f(x)=\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\,1}f(x)$\n\n![](images/68/68241f3cd913cd91d10765499dc4ce4398fb3e8a81ec4bcfb65b47c0d77731d6.png)" 13194139535509 17592186045417 false] [17592186068534 1071 "Which of the following statements are true concerning the function $y=f(x)$ whose graph is shown below? \n\nI. $\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\,0}f(x)=0$\n\nII. $\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\,3}f(x)=0$\n\nIII. $\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\,-1}f(x)=\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\,1}f(x)$\n\n![](images/68/68241f3cd913cd91d10765499dc4ce4398fb3e8a81ec4bcfb65b47c0d77731d6.png)" 13194139535509 17592186045417 true] [17592186068534 1074 "Let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. As $x$ approaches $0$, the function $f(x)$ approaches the value $y=-1$. Consequently,\n$\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\,0}f(x)=-1$.\n- Statement II is true. As $x$ approaches $3$, the function $f(x)$ approaches the value $y=0$. Consequently,\n$\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\,3}f(x)=0$.\n- Statement III is false. As $x$ approaches $-1$ the function $f(x)$ approaches the value $y=2$, and consequently\n$\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\,-1}f(x)=2$.\nSimiliarly, as $x$ approaches $1$, the function $f(x)$ approaches the value $y=-2$, and consequently\n$\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\,1}f(x)=-2$.\nAs a result,\n$\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\,-1}f(x)\\ne\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\,1}f(x)$.\n\nIn conclusion, only statement II is true." 13194139535509 17592186045417 false] [17592186068534 1074 "Let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. As $x$ approaches $0,$ the function $f(x)$ approaches the value $y=-1.$ Consequently, \n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\,0}f(x)=-1.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. As $x$ approaches $3,$ the function $f(x)$ approaches the value $y=0.$ Consequently,\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\,3}f(x)=0.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. As $x$ approaches $-1$ the function $f(x)$ approaches the value $y=2,$ and consequently\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\,-1}f(x)=2.\n$$\n\nSimiliarly, as $x$ approaches $1,$ the function $f(x)$ approaches the value $y=-2,$ and consequently\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\,1}f(x)=-2.\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\,-1}f(x)\\ne\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\,1}f(x).\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, only statement II is true." 13194139535509 17592186045417 true] [17592186068791 1038 #edb/ref 17592186510368 13194139535509 17592186045417 true] [17592186068791 1038 #edb/ref 17592186510431 13194139535509 17592186045417 true] [17592186068791 1038 #edb/ref 17592186510453 13194139535509 17592186045417 true] [17592186068791 1038 #edb/ref 17592186510473 13194139535509 17592186045417 true] [17592186068988 1050 420.0 13194139535509 17592186045417 true] [17592186069924 1064 "The limit of a function at some point has **nothing to do** with the value of the function at that point.\n\nFor example, the following functions $g(x)$ and $h(x)$ have *different values* at $x=2$, but they have the *same limit* at $x=2$.\n\n![](images/6a/6a73b74072a8a990c91a38553d519f4913370d34ef0251b7e45cc664da40517d.png)\n\n![](images/06/06e150aacae774b4fe883a2750ef13cebbddf2ea871e5a682f2587c47a54615a.png)\n\nThe actual values of $g(2)$ and $h(2)$ are irrelevant for determining the limit at $x=2$. All that matters is what values the functions *look like* they are approaching as $x$ approaches $2$.\n\nNear $x=2$, both $g(x)$ and $h(x)$ appear to be approaching $y=1$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow 2}g(x)=1\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow 2}h(x)=1 \\,\n$$" 13194139535509 17592186045417 false] [17592186069924 1064 "The limit of a function at some point has **nothing to do** with the value of the function at that point.\n\nFor example, the following functions $g(x)$ and $h(x)$ have *different values* at $x=2,$ but they have the *same limit* at $x=2.$\n\n![](images/6a/6a73b74072a8a990c91a38553d519f4913370d34ef0251b7e45cc664da40517d.png)\n\n![](images/06/06e150aacae774b4fe883a2750ef13cebbddf2ea871e5a682f2587c47a54615a.png)\n\nThe actual values of $g(2)$ and $h(2)$ are irrelevant for determining the limit at $x=2$. All that matters is what values the functions *look like* they are approaching as $x$ approaches $2$. \n\nNear $x=2,$ both $g(x)$ and $h(x)$ appear to be approaching $y=1$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow 2}g(x)=1\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow 2}h(x)=1 \\, .\n$$" 13194139535509 17592186045417 true] [17592186069924 1067 "Limit Has Nothing to Do With the Function Value" 13194139535509 17592186045417 true] [17592186069924 1067 "Limit Has Nothing to Do with Function Value" 13194139535509 17592186045417 false] [17592186069928 1064 "The **limit** of a function is the value that the function approaches. For example, the function $y=f(x)$ shown below is not defined at $x=2$, as represented by the open circle there $-$ but when we *approach* $x=2$, we see that $f(x)$ *approaches* the value $y=1$.\n\n![](images/f7/f7081d649c6cdfa0a19a0fb2de93b97ee700ad922f34530c7f3e5c2a3228c9fb.png)\n\nAs $x$ approaches $2$, the value of $f(x)$ approaches $1$. In other words, the limit of $f(x)$, as $x$ approaches $2$, is $1$. We can express this mathematically as\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow 2}f(x)=1 \\,\n$$" 13194139535509 17592186045417 false] [17592186069928 1064 "The **limit** of a function is the value that the function approaches. For example, the function $y=f(x)$ shown below is not defined at $x=2,$ as represented by the open circle there $-$ but when we *approach* $x=2$, we see that $f(x)$ *approaches* the value $y=1.$\n\n![](images/f7/f7081d649c6cdfa0a19a0fb2de93b97ee700ad922f34530c7f3e5c2a3228c9fb.png)\n\nAs $x$ approaches $2$, the value of $f(x)$ approaches $1.$ In other words, the limit of $f(x),$ as $x$ approaches $2,$ is $1$. We can express this mathematically as \n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow 2}f(x)=1 \\, .\n$$" 13194139535509 17592186045417 true] [17592186073686 1038 #edb/ref 17592186510444 13194139535509 17592186045417 true] [17592186073708 1038 #edb/ref 17592186510460 13194139535509 17592186045417 true] [17592186073718 1038 #edb/ref 17592186510435 13194139535509 17592186045417 true] [17592186073721 1038 #edb/ref 17592186510434 13194139535509 17592186045417 true] [17592186073722 1038 #edb/ref 17592186510433 13194139535509 17592186045417 true] [17592186073722 1038 #edb/ref 17592186510455 13194139535509 17592186045417 true] [17592186073723 1038 #edb/ref 17592186510390 13194139535509 17592186045417 true] [17592186073796 1038 #edb/ref 17592186510370 13194139535509 17592186045417 true] [17592186073902 1038 #edb/ref 17592186510394 13194139535509 17592186045417 true] [17592186077654 1078 #edb/ref 17592186077657 13194139535509 17592186045417 true] [17592186078527 1038 #edb/ref 17592186510375 13194139535509 17592186045417 true] [17592186078528 1038 #edb/ref 17592186510456 13194139535509 17592186045417 true] [17592186079146 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535509 17592186045417 true] [17592186079146 1074 "A rule is a function if and only if *every* input is mapped to *precisely one* output.\n\nIn particular, a rule is *not* a function in the following cases:\n\n- An input value is not assigned to an output value. For example, the following situation is *not allowed* for functions (an input value with no arrow from it):\n\n![](images/bb/bb53865d210d631d78e5ce48aa96ecff99da8995936a085c789c3471ffb9e6a0.png)\n\n \n\n- An input value is assigned to more than one output value. For example, the following situation is *not allowed* for functions (an input value with more than one arrow from it):\n\n![](images/18/18831dc73e561faa69748fed111eb7e1024d35849fb6624594ae08d8c35301ee.png)\n\n \n\nIn this particular example, the rule *is* a function because *each* input value is assigned *precisely one* output value." 13194139535509 17592186045417 true] [17592186093686 1078 #edb/ref 17592186093687 13194139535509 17592186045417 true] [17592186094987 1078 #edb/ref 17592186094988 13194139535509 17592186045417 true] [17592186096438 1071 "Consider a vector $\\textbf{a}$ and a point $O$ (shown below). Draw a vector that is equal to the vector $\\textbf{a}$ and has its tail at $O$.\n\n![](images/57/571e2d2a8ffab997a13f99973a8605c5b9b410439239a3acef5a7fd1dac939ae.png)" 13194139535509 17592186045417 false] [17592186096438 1071 "Consider a vector $\\textbf{a}$ and a point $O$ (shown below). Draw a vector that is equal to the vector $\\textbf{a}$ and has its tail at $O.$\n\n![](images/57/571e2d2a8ffab997a13f99973a8605c5b9b410439239a3acef5a7fd1dac939ae.png)" 13194139535509 17592186045417 true] [17592186096438 1074 "First, we sketch the line that is passing through $O$ and parallel to $\\textbf{a}$.\n\n![](images/1c/1c1acea46337104f17184784dfc9fecc21360112fc510ac56665565e1c800804.png)\n\nNow, we choose a point $B$ on the line, in the direction of $\\textbf{a}$, such that $OB$ equals the length of $\\textbf{a}$.\n\n![](images/09/0931711b7da5d109085f729999016f0b7c7fb7930f658a909739910c37d05ce8.png)\n\nSo, the vector $\\overrightarrow{OB}$ is equal to the vector $\\textbf{a}$ and has its tail at $O$." 13194139535509 17592186045417 false] [17592186096438 1074 "First, we sketch the line that is passing through $O$ and parallel to $\\textbf{a}.$\n\n![](images/1c/1c1acea46337104f17184784dfc9fecc21360112fc510ac56665565e1c800804.png)\n\nNow, we choose a point $B$ on the line, in the direction of $\\textbf{a},$ such that $OB$ equals the length of $\\textbf{a}.$\n\n![](images/09/0931711b7da5d109085f729999016f0b7c7fb7930f658a909739910c37d05ce8.png)\n\nSo, the vector $\\overrightarrow{OB}$ is equal to the vector $\\textbf{a}$ and has its tail at $O.$" 13194139535509 17592186045417 true] [17592186096439 1071 "The vectors $\\textbf{a}$ and $\\textbf{b}$ are shown in the diagram below. Draw $\\textbf{a}+\\textbf{b}$.\n\n![](images/31/31170f98fe0a33f2f83a32240899f7274710d8735816cf4626585c411b984a67.png)" 13194139535509 17592186045417 false] [17592186096439 1071 "The vectors $\\textbf{a}$ and $\\textbf{b}$ are shown in the diagram below. Draw $\\textbf{a}+\\textbf{b}.$\n\n![](images/31/31170f98fe0a33f2f83a32240899f7274710d8735816cf4626585c411b984a67.png)" 13194139535509 17592186045417 true] [17592186096439 1074 "First, we draw the vector that is equal to $\\mathbf{b}$ and has its tail placed at the head of $\\mathbf{a}$.\n\nNow, according to the triangle law of addition, the resultant\n$\\mathbf{a}+\\mathbf{b}$\nis the vector that goes from the tail of $\\textbf{a}$ to the head of $\\textbf{b}$, as shown below.\n\n![](images/3a/3af74d7b5cae7b1aa2b8f5ee5cfcb52b1a048316f214436ac953de0062c31f92.png)" 13194139535509 17592186045417 false] [17592186096439 1074 "First, we draw the vector that is equal to $\\mathbf{b}$ and has its tail placed at the head of $\\mathbf{a}.$ \n\nNow, according to the triangle law of addition, the resultant \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a}+\\mathbf{b}\n$$\n\nis the vector that goes from the tail of $\\textbf{a}$ to the head of $\\textbf{b}$, as shown below.\n\n![](images/3a/3af74d7b5cae7b1aa2b8f5ee5cfcb52b1a048316f214436ac953de0062c31f92.png)" 13194139535509 17592186045417 true] [17592186096441 1071 "Consider the vector $\\overrightarrow{AB}$, shown below. What is the resultant of $\\overrightarrow{AB}+\\overrightarrow{BA}$?\n\n![](images/17/17531cbc767566b8c8d60536372001b2492d44456e818498e8edb2600471db49.png)" 13194139535509 17592186045417 false] [17592186096441 1071 "Consider the vector $\\overrightarrow{AB},$ shown below. What is the resultant of $\\overrightarrow{AB}+\\overrightarrow{BA}?$\n\n![](images/17/17531cbc767566b8c8d60536372001b2492d44456e818498e8edb2600471db49.png)" 13194139535509 17592186045417 true] [17592186096441 1074 "According to the triangle law of addition, the resultant is the vector that goes from the tail of $\\overrightarrow{AB}$ to the head of $\\overrightarrow{BA}$. Therefore, we obtain a new vector for which the tail and head are the same point, $A$.\n\nThat vector is called the zero vector, and we write\n\n$$\n\\overrightarrow{AB}+\\overrightarrow{BA} = \\overrightarrow{AA}=\\mathbf{0}\n$$" 13194139535509 17592186045417 false] [17592186096441 1074 "According to the triangle law of addition, the resultant is the vector that goes from the tail of $\\overrightarrow{AB}$ to the head of $\\overrightarrow{BA}.$ Therefore, we obtain a new vector for which the tail and head are the same point, $A.$ \n\nThat vector is called the zero vector, and we write\n\n$$\n\\overrightarrow{AB}+\\overrightarrow{BA} = \\overrightarrow{AA}=\\mathbf{0}.\n$$" 13194139535509 17592186045417 true] [17592186096443 1071 "The vectors $\\textbf{a}$, $\\textbf{b}$, and $\\textbf{c}$ are shown in the diagram below. Find $\\textbf{a}+\\textbf{b}-\\textbf{c}$.\n\n![](images/96/9665262608454d38d6a442715345cc014d40ca2e3d9a7cdf7c8cd0b8fc00f0d7.png)" 13194139535509 17592186045417 false] [17592186096443 1071 "The vectors $\\textbf{a},$ $\\textbf{b},$ and $\\textbf{c}$ are shown in the diagram below. Find $\\textbf{a}+\\textbf{b}-\\textbf{c}.$\n\n![](images/96/9665262608454d38d6a442715345cc014d40ca2e3d9a7cdf7c8cd0b8fc00f0d7.png)" 13194139535509 17592186045417 true] [17592186096443 1074 "First, we use the triangle law of addition to find\n$\\textbf{a}+\\textbf{b}$.\nThe resultant $\\mathbf{a}+\\mathbf{b}$ is the vector that joins the tail of $\\textbf{a}$ and the head of $\\textbf{b}$:\n\n![](images/26/2643b94a575f1b2cd9967e530019b8b100928b2f9888aaf833dd84423fbe6e10.png)\n\nNow, we use the triangle law of addition to find\n$(\\textbf{a}+\\textbf{b})+(-\\textbf{c})$.\nWe draw $-\\textbf{c}$ from the head of $\\textbf{a}+\\textbf{b}$. So, we have the vector that goes from the tail of $\\textbf{a}$ to the head of $-\\textbf{c}$:\n\n![](images/d7/d77ad19b87fe8afba44663482ed6e8adfb2f251c4439c488ab7b3cde8b4a60ac.png)" 13194139535509 17592186045417 false] [17592186096443 1074 "First, we use the triangle law of addition to find \n\n$$\n\\textbf{a}+\\textbf{b}.\n$$\n\nThe resultant $\\mathbf{a}+\\mathbf{b}$ is the vector that joins the tail of $\\textbf{a}$ and the head of $\\textbf{b}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/26/2643b94a575f1b2cd9967e530019b8b100928b2f9888aaf833dd84423fbe6e10.png)\n\nNow, we use the triangle law of addition to find \n\n$$\n(\\textbf{a}+\\textbf{b})+(-\\textbf{c}).\n$$\n\nWe draw $-\\textbf{c}$ from the head of $\\textbf{a}+\\textbf{b}.$ So, we have the vector that goes from the tail of $\\textbf{a}$ to the head of $-\\textbf{c}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/d7/d77ad19b87fe8afba44663482ed6e8adfb2f251c4439c488ab7b3cde8b4a60ac.png)" 13194139535509 17592186045417 true] [17592186096453 1071 "Consider the diagram that shows the vectors $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$. Draw $\\mathbf{a}-\\mathbf{b}$.\n\n![](images/a7/a7ed4f4f82c61679a81ce151335695f9ba226529aafe79a88700430e0594fecd.png)" 13194139535509 17592186045417 false] [17592186096453 1071 "Consider the diagram that shows the vectors $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$. Draw $\\mathbf{a}-\\mathbf{b}.$\n\n![](images/a7/a7ed4f4f82c61679a81ce151335695f9ba226529aafe79a88700430e0594fecd.png)" 13194139535509 17592186045417 true] [17592186096453 1074 "We interpret the subtraction\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a}-\\mathbf{b}\n$$\n\nas the addition\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a} + (-\\mathbf{b}),\n$$\n\nwhere $-\\mathbf{b}$ is the vector that has the same magnitude as $\\mathbf{b}$ but goes in the direction *opposite* of $\\mathbf{b}.$\n\nFinally, we use the triangle law to add the vectors $\\mathbf{a}$ and $-\\mathbf{b}\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n- We can start $\\mathbf{a}$ from an arbitrary point on the plane.\n\n- Next, we draw the vector that is equal to $\\mathbf{-b}$ and has its tail placed at the head of $\\mathbf{a}.$\n\n- Now, according to the triangle law of addition, the resultant is the vector that goes from the tail of $\\textbf{a}$ to the head of $-\\textbf{b}$, as shown below.\n\n![](images/1d/1d5ad4a8f48ccdaefcd9b6ae91ef533c6a77f5800e2adede362840700acd7785.png)" 13194139535509 17592186045417 true] [17592186096453 1074 "We interpret the subtraction\n$\\mathbf{a}-\\mathbf{b}$\nas the addition\n$\\mathbf{a} + (-\\mathbf{b})$,\nwhere $-\\mathbf{b}$ is the vector that has the same magnitude as $\\mathbf{b}$ but goes in the direction *opposite* of $\\mathbf{b}$.\n\nFinally, we use the triangle law to add the vectors $\\mathbf{a}$ and $-\\mathbf{b}$:\n\n- We can start $\\mathbf{a}$ from an arbitrary point on the plane.\n- Next, we draw the vector that is equal to $\\mathbf{-b}$ and has its tail placed at the head of $\\mathbf{a}$.\n- Now, according to the triangle law of addition, the resultant is the vector that goes from the tail of $\\textbf{a}$ to the head of $-\\textbf{b}$, as shown below.\n\n![](images/1d/1d5ad4a8f48ccdaefcd9b6ae91ef533c6a77f5800e2adede362840700acd7785.png)" 13194139535509 17592186045417 false] [17592186096484 1074 "Two vectors are equal if and only if they lie on parallel lines and have both the same magnitude and the same direction.\n\nThe vectors $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{e}$ are parallel and have the same direction and magnitude, hence\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a} = \\mathbf{e}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, $\\mathbf{b}$ and $\\mathbf{d}$ are parallel and have the same direction and magnitude, hence\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{b} = \\mathbf{d}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand,\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a} \\neq \\mathbf{b}\n$$\n\n,\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a} \\neq \\mathbf{c}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{b} \\neq \\mathbf{c}\n$$\n\nbecause they are not parallel.\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a}=\\mathbf{e}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{b}=\\mathbf{d}\n$$\n\nonly.\n\n![](images/03/03ef54909ccfcd9c8e1f85746ec5b981e6a88c1fbf1f0c6c2337b19b05c694a7.png)" 13194139535509 17592186045417 false] [17592186096484 1074 "Two vectors are equal if and only if they lie on parallel lines and have both the same magnitude and the same direction.\n\nThe vectors $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{e}$ are parallel and have the same direction and magnitude, hence $\\mathbf{a} = \\mathbf{e}.$ Similarly, $\\mathbf{b}$ and $\\mathbf{d}$ are parallel and have the same direction and magnitude, hence $\\mathbf{b} = \\mathbf{d}.$\n\nOn the other hand, $\\mathbf{a} \\neq \\mathbf{b}$, $\\mathbf{a} \\neq \\mathbf{c}$ and $\\mathbf{b} \\neq \\mathbf{c}$ because they are not parallel.\n\nTherefore, we get $\\mathbf{a}=\\mathbf{e}$ and $\\mathbf{b}=\\mathbf{d}$ only.\n\n![](images/03/03ef54909ccfcd9c8e1f85746ec5b981e6a88c1fbf1f0c6c2337b19b05c694a7.png)" 13194139535509 17592186045417 true] [17592186096640 1038 #edb/ref 17592186510369 13194139535509 17592186045417 true] [17592186096641 1038 #edb/ref 17592186510393 13194139535509 17592186045417 true] [17592186096642 1038 #edb/ref 17592186510432 13194139535509 17592186045417 true] [17592186096642 1038 #edb/ref 17592186510474 13194139535509 17592186045417 true] [17592186096644 1038 #edb/ref 17592186510410 13194139535509 17592186045417 true] [17592186096644 1038 #edb/ref 17592186510477 13194139535509 17592186045417 true] [17592186096646 1038 #edb/ref 17592186510440 13194139535509 17592186045417 true] [17592186096646 1038 #edb/ref 17592186510459 13194139535509 17592186045417 true] [17592186096654 1038 #edb/ref 17592186510404 13194139535509 17592186045417 true] [17592186096654 1038 #edb/ref 17592186510437 13194139535509 17592186045417 true] [17592186096689 1038 #edb/ref 17592186510377 13194139535509 17592186045417 true] [17592186096689 1038 #edb/ref 17592186510382 13194139535509 17592186045417 true] [17592186096880 1050 480.0 13194139535509 17592186045417 true] [17592186096887 1050 420.0 13194139535509 17592186045417 true] [17592186096952 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535509 17592186045417 true] [17592186096952 1071 "Which of the diagrams shows the displacement vector of a ship that travels $10$ $\\text{km}$ on a bearing of ${60}^{∘}?$" 13194139535509 17592186045417 true] [17592186096952 1071 "Which of the diagrams shows the displacement vector of a ship that travels $10$ $km$ on a bearing of $60^{∘}$?" 13194139535509 17592186045417 false] [17592186096952 1074 "We choose an arbitrary point on the plane as the starting point (tail) of the vector. The vertical line shows the north direction.\n\n![](images/60/604ccf740830c570ee87731a719165cca75d615b3a4c8861d2671ccac95aad2e.png)\n\nWe go ${60}^{∘}$ clockwise from the north. \n\n![](images/7d/7d90ee7f259bff7244af1e3709f3ea5bdbd67ec10be795b15f5526e03221a6ab.png)\n\nThe length of the line segment of the vector represents the displacement of the ship (which is $10$ $\\text{km}$)." 13194139535509 17592186045417 true] [17592186097169 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535509 17592186045417 true] [17592186097169 1071 "![](images/b9/b968f83df851f5b19e7073192d7d65f938440be62c60859f553b33b75c852170.png)\n\nConsider the vector $\\text{a}$ and a point $O$ shown above. Which of the following diagrams shows the vector that is equal to the vector $\\text{a}$ and has its tail at $O.$" 13194139535509 17592186045417 true] [17592186097169 1071 "![](images/b9/b968f83df851f5b19e7073192d7d65f938440be62c60859f553b33b75c852170.png)\n\nConsider the vector $a$ and a point $O$ shown above. Which of the following diagrams shows the vector that is equal to the vector $a$ and has its tail at $O$." 13194139535509 17592186045417 false] [17592186097169 1074 "First, we sketch the line that is passing through $O$ and parallel to $\\text{a}.$\n\n![](images/12/12579c5ee42e21b0a312f628bda36992fc343ace38dfbc2758a2e3d6aed49124.png)\n\nNow, we choose a point $A$ on the line, in the direction of $\\text{a},$ such that $OA$ equals the length of $\\text{a}.$\n\n![](images/cd/cdda8763ddc663d090099a30731d212a076b24063c7fdfeef9c0f7d9505709e1.png)\n\nSo, $\\overset{\\to }{OA}=\\text{a}.$" 13194139535509 17592186045417 true] [17592186097826 1064 "A **vector** is a mathematical object that is used to represent quantities that have both **direction** and **magnitude.** We can think of a vector as a \"directed line segment\".\n\nFor example, consider a ship that travels $15\\:\\textrm{km}$ due east. In the diagram below, the ship starts at the point $A$ and ends at the point $B$. This information can be represented as a **vector**, a directed line segment that starts at $A$ and points to $B$, as follows:\n\n![](images/5c/5c12cfd2657f42293b2ee53fd2a4aede2f0325233305be9ac9bc0b5620391654.png)\n\nThe vector itself represents the **displacement** of the ship. The magnitude (length) of the vector represents the total distance traveled, and the direction of the vector gives the direction of travel.\n\nThe **tail** of the vector is at the point $A$. The **head** of the vector is at the point $B$.\n\nThe vector can be written using its endpoints as $\\overrightarrow{AB}$. We can also represent vectors using lowercase letters written in bold, such as $\\mathbf{a}$, $\\mathbf{b}$, $\\mathbf{c}$." 13194139535509 17592186045417 false] [17592186097826 1064 "A **vector** is a mathematical object that is used to represent quantities that have both **direction** and **magnitude.** We can think of a vector as a \"directed line segment\".\n\nFor example, consider a ship that travels $15\\:\\textrm{km}$ due east. In the diagram below, the ship starts at the point $A$ and ends at the point $B.$ This information can be represented as a **vector**, a directed line segment that starts at $A$ and points to $B,$ as follows:\n\n![](images/5c/5c12cfd2657f42293b2ee53fd2a4aede2f0325233305be9ac9bc0b5620391654.png)\n\nThe vector itself represents the **displacement** of the ship. The magnitude (length) of the vector represents the total distance traveled, and the direction of the vector gives the direction of travel.\n\nThe **tail** of the vector is at the point $A.$ The **head** of the vector is at the point $B.$ \n\nThe vector can be written using its endpoints as $\\overrightarrow{AB}.$ We can also represent vectors using lowercase letters written in bold, such as $\\mathbf{a},$ $\\mathbf{b},$ $\\mathbf{c}.$" 13194139535509 17592186045417 true] [17592186097827 1064 "Two vectors are **equal** if they have both the same magnitude and the same direction. For example, the two vectors shown in the picture below are equal.\n\n![](images/2a/2a0d9e89849dcedc7dcd8395fcf1379a1f857cf1a6e91e490489d93d08a3da1d.png)\n\nAs we can see from the picture above, we have\n\n$$\n\\overrightarrow{AB} = \\overrightarrow{CD} = \\textbf{a}\n$$" 13194139535509 17592186045417 false] [17592186097827 1064 "Two vectors are **equal** if they have both the same magnitude and the same direction. For example, the two vectors shown in the picture below are equal.\n\n![](images/2a/2a0d9e89849dcedc7dcd8395fcf1379a1f857cf1a6e91e490489d93d08a3da1d.png)\n\nAs we can see from the picture above, we have\n\n$$\n\\overrightarrow{AB} = \\overrightarrow{CD} = \\textbf{a}.\n$$" 13194139535509 17592186045417 true] [17592186097828 1064 "Given two vectors, we can compute the sum by placing the head of one vector at the tail of the other.\n\nFor example, let's consider the two vectors $\\textbf{a}$ and $\\textbf{b}$ below.\n\n![](images/04/049f79ae22e5931a7ceba7f73fef5cb6bc38d17c2a45e7186d57f8760b02488a.png)\n\nTo find $\\mathbf{a}+\\mathbf{b}$, we need to draw the second vector $\\textbf{b}$ starting from the head of the first vector $\\textbf{a}$. Now, the sum $\\mathbf{a}+\\mathbf{b}$ is the vector that we get when we join the tail of $\\textbf{a}$ to the head of $\\textbf{b}$:\n\n![](images/98/98a42bc3ecf7071630acc7d29349f75f77b20a9cc1c2c34382161cc79b47b4df.png)\n\nWe call this method the **triangle law of addition**. The vector obtained by adding two or more vectors is sometimes called the **resultant**." 13194139535509 17592186045417 false] [17592186097828 1064 "Given two vectors, we can compute the sum by placing the head of one vector at the tail of the other.\n\nFor example, let's consider the two vectors $\\textbf{a}$ and $\\textbf{b}$ below.\n\n![](images/04/049f79ae22e5931a7ceba7f73fef5cb6bc38d17c2a45e7186d57f8760b02488a.png)\n\nTo find $\\mathbf{a}+\\mathbf{b},$ we need to draw the second vector $\\textbf{b}$ starting from the head of the first vector $\\textbf{a}.$ Now, the sum $\\mathbf{a}+\\mathbf{b}$ is the vector that we get when we join the tail of $\\textbf{a}$ to the head of $\\textbf{b}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/98/98a42bc3ecf7071630acc7d29349f75f77b20a9cc1c2c34382161cc79b47b4df.png)\n\nWe call this method the **triangle law of addition**. The vector obtained by adding two or more vectors is sometimes called the **resultant**." 13194139535509 17592186045417 true] [17592186097830 1064 "Given a vector $\\overrightarrow{AB}$, the **opposite vector** is the vector $\\overrightarrow{BA}$ that has the same length but goes in the opposite direction.\n\n![](images/65/6591fd004ba2b74c2651182ce0c70302ac789d868389d057a502b8e98ac61fab.png)\n\nWe write\n\n$$\n\\overrightarrow{AB}=-\\overrightarrow{BA}\n$$\n\nTo compute the sum of two opposite vectors,\n$\\overrightarrow{AB}+\\overrightarrow{BA}$,\nwe can use the triangle law of addition.\n\nAccording to the triangle law of addition, the resultant is the vector that goes from the tail of $\\overrightarrow{AB}$ to the head of $\\overrightarrow{BA}$. Therefore, we obtain a new vector for which the tail and head are the same point, $A$. We denote that vector as\n\n$$\n\\overrightarrow{AB}+\\overrightarrow{BA} = \\overrightarrow{AA}=\\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nThe vector $\\mathbf{0}$ is called the **zero vector**. The zero vector has no direction and zero magnitude. With the zero vector, the head coincides with the tail." 13194139535509 17592186045417 false] [17592186097830 1064 "Given a vector $\\overrightarrow{AB},$ the **opposite vector** is the vector $\\overrightarrow{BA}$ that has the same length but goes in the opposite direction.\n\n![](images/65/6591fd004ba2b74c2651182ce0c70302ac789d868389d057a502b8e98ac61fab.png)\n\nWe write\n\n$$\n\\overrightarrow{AB}=-\\overrightarrow{BA}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the sum of two opposite vectors,\n\n$$\n\\overrightarrow{AB}+\\overrightarrow{BA},\n$$\n\nwe can use the triangle law of addition.\n\nAccording to the triangle law of addition, the resultant is the vector that goes from the tail of $\\overrightarrow{AB}$ to the head of $\\overrightarrow{BA}.$ Therefore, we obtain a new vector for which the tail and head are the same point, $A.$ We denote that vector as\n\n$$\n\\overrightarrow{AB}+\\overrightarrow{BA} = \\overrightarrow{AA}=\\mathbf{0}.\n$$\n\nThe vector $\\mathbf{0}$ is called the **zero vector**. The zero vector has no direction and zero magnitude. With the zero vector, the head coincides with the tail." 13194139535509 17592186045417 true] [17592186097838 1064 "We've seen how to use the triangle law to add two vectors. We can also use the triangle law to subtract two vectors.\n\nThe trick is to interpret the subtraction as the addition of a negative vector. For example, the subtraction\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a}-\\mathbf{b}\n$$\n\ncan be interpreted as the addition\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a} + (-\\mathbf{b}),\n$$\n\nwhere $-\\mathbf{b}$ is the vector that has the same magnitude as $\\mathbf{b}$ but goes in the direction *opposite* of $\\mathbf{b}.$\n\nLet's see a concrete example." 13194139535509 17592186045417 true] [17592186097838 1064 "We've seen how to use the triangle law to add two vectors. We can also use the triangle law to subtract two vectors.\n\nThe trick is to interpret the subtraction as the addition of a negative vector. For example, the subtraction\n$\\mathbf{a}-\\mathbf{b}$\ncan be interpreted as the addition\n$\\mathbf{a} + (-\\mathbf{b})$,\nwhere $-\\mathbf{b}$ is the vector that has the same magnitude as $\\mathbf{b}$ but goes in the direction *opposite* of $\\mathbf{b}$.\n\nLet's see a concrete example." 13194139535509 17592186045417 false] [17592186098709 1078 #edb/ref 17592186098714 13194