 we need to differentiate $S$ and set the derivative equal to zero. The derivative is\n\n$$\nS'(r) = -\\dfrac{54\\pi}{r^2} + 2\\pi r\n$$\n\nSetting the derivative equal to zero and solving for $r$ gives a stationary point, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nS^{′}(r) &= 0 \\\\\n- \\frac{54π}{r^{2}} + 2πr &= 0 \\\\\n2πr &= \\frac{54π}{r^{2}} \\\\\n2πr^{3} &= 54π \\\\\nr^{3} &= 27 \\\\\nr &= \\sqrt[3]{27} \\\\\nr &= 3in\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing a calculator, we can verify that if $r < 3$ then $S'(r) < 0$, and if $r > 3$ then $S'(r) > 0$, which means that the stationary point $r=3$ is indeed a minimum of $S(r)$.\n\nTherefore, the cylinder with the smallest surface area has radius\n\n$$\nr =3\\,\\textrm{in}\n$$" 13194139535509 17592186045417 false] [17592186100650 1074 "First, let's draw a picture of a cylinder. Let $r$ be the radius, and $h$ the height.\n\n![](images/ad/adb9c56132e8f346a07a9a644cd364c2347ad8c551a3c9bd4842b7067eb1d050.png)\n\nWe wish to minimize the surface area\n\n$$\nS= 2\\pi r h + \\pi r^2.\n$$\n\nWe know that the volume is fixed at $27\\pi \\, \\textrm{in}^3,$ so we also have the constraint condition\n\n$$\n27\\pi = \\pi r^2 h.\n$$\n\nNow, by making $h$ the subject of the constraint equation, we have that\n\n$$\nh = \\dfrac{27}{ r^2}.\n$$\n\nPlugging the above into our expression for $S,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\nS(r)& = 2\\pi r \\left(\\dfrac{27}{ r^2}\\right) + \\pi r^2\\\n & = \\dfrac{54\\pi}{r} +  \\pi r^2.}\n$$\n\nWe want to minimize $S,$ so we need to differentiate $S$ and set the derivative equal to zero. The derivative is \n\n$$\nS'(r) = -\\dfrac{54\\pi}{r^2} + 2\\pi r.\n$$\n\nSetting the derivative equal to zero and solving for $r$ gives a stationary point, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(r) & =0 \\\\ -\\frac{54\\pi }{{r}^{2}}+2\\pi r & =0 \\\\ 2\\pi r & =\\frac{54\\pi }{{r}^{2}} \\\\ 2\\pi {r}^{3} & =54\\pi  \\\\ {r}^{3} & =27 \\\\ r & =\\sqrt[3]{27} \\\\ r & =3\\,\\text{in}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing a calculator, we c