\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535509 17592186045417 true] [17592186101277 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535509 17592186045417 true] [17592186101277 1071 "![](images/50/50853fcaa44ff9b7d80e708e546275286541afc0b6ed05c4ca87ea3175364c09.png)\n\nWe need to create a cylindrical tin with no lid using $48\\pi \\,{cm}^{2}$ of sheet metal. What is the largest possible volume of the cylinder?" 13194139535509 17592186045417 true] [17592186101277 1071 "![](images/50/50853fcaa44ff9b7d80e708e546275286541afc0b6ed05c4ca87ea3175364c09.png)\n\nWe need to create a cylindrical tin with no lid using $48πcm^{2}$ of sheet metal. What is the largest possible volume of the cylinder?\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139535509 17592186045417 false] [17592186101277 1074 "First, let $r$ be the radius and $h$ be the height of the cylinder.\n\nWe wish to maximize the volume\n\n$$\nV=\\pi {r}^{2}h.\n$$\n\nWe know that the surface area is fixed at $48\\pi \\,{cm}^{2},$ so we also have the constraint equation\n\n$$\n2\\pi rh+\\pi {r}^{2}=48\\pi .\n$$\n\nNow, by making $h$ the subject of the constraint equation, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\pi rh & =48\\pi -\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\ h & =\\frac{48\\pi -\\pi {r}^{2}}{2\\pi r} \\\\ h & =\\frac{48-{r}^{2}}{2r}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging the above into our expression for $V,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V(r) & =\\pi {r}^{2}\\times \\frac{48-{r}^{2}}{2r} \\\\ V(r) & =\\frac{(48r-{r}^{3})\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to maximize $V,$ so we need to differentiate $V$ and set the derivative equal to zero. The derivative is \n\n$$\n{V}^{′}(r)=\\frac{(48-3{r}^{2})\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nSetting the derivative equal to zero and solving for $r$ gives a stationary point, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}^{′}(r) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{(48-3{r}^{2})\\pi }{2} & =0 \\\\ 48 & =3{r}^{2} \\\\ {r}^{2} & =16 \\\\ r & =4\\,\\text{cm}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that, in the last step, we only considered the positive root because the radius $r$ must be positive.\n\nFinally, we confirm that $r=4$ is a maximum using the second derivative test. The second derivative ${V}^{″}(r)$ is\n\n$$\n{V}^{″}(r)=\\frac{-6\\pi r}{2}=-3\\pi r,\n$$\n\nand we can see that ${V}^{″}(4)<0.$ So, the stationary point $r=4$ is indeed a maximum of $V(r).$\n\nSo the cylinder with the largest volume has a radius of $r=4\\,\\text{cm}.$\n\nFinally, to find the largest volume, we substitute $r=4\\,\\text{cm}$ into the equation for the volume $V:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V(4) & =\\frac{(48(4)-(4)^{3})\\pi }{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(192-64)\\pi }{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{128\\pi }{2} \\\\  & =64\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the largest possible volume of the cylinder is $V=64\\pi \\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}.$" 13194139535509 17592186045417 true] [17592186101278 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535509 17592186045417 true] [17592186101278 1071 "![](images/38/38883c7cc4bfe8c9ecccadfcc2d0c75ff66f79b19ff697c15ddbe53f4c63dbab.png)\n\nWe need to create a cylindrical tin with a lid using $216\\pi \\,{cm}^{2}$ of sheet metal. What is the radius $r$ of the cylinder that has the largest volume?" 13194139535509 17592186045417 true] [17592186101278 1071 "![](images/38/38883c7cc4bfe8c9ecccadfcc2d0c75ff66f79b19ff697c15ddbe53f4c63dbab.png)\n\nWe need to create a cylindrical tin with a lid using $216πcm^{2}$ of sheet metal. What is the radius $r$ of the cylinder that has the largest volume?\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139535509 17592186045417 false] [17592186101278 1074 "First, let $r$ be the radius and $h$ be the height of the cylinder.\n\nWe wish to maximize the volume\n\n$$\nV=\\pi {r}^{2}h.\n$$\n\nWe know that the surface area is fixed at $216\\pi \\,{cm}^{2},$ so we also have the constraint equation\n\n$$\n2\\pi rh+2\\pi {r}^{2}=216\\pi .\n$$\n\nNow, by making $h$ the subject of the constraint equation, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\pi rh & =216\\pi -2\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\ h & =\\frac{216\\pi -2\\pi {r}^{2}}{2\\pi r} \\\\ h & =\\frac{108-{r}^{2}}{r}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging the above into our expression for $V,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V(r) & =\\pi {r}^{2}\\times \\frac{108-{r}^{2}}{r} \\\\ V(r) & =(108r-{r}^{3})\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to maximize $V,$ so we need to differentiate $V$ and set the derivative equal to zero. The derivative is \n\n$$\n{V}^{′}(r)=(108-3{r}^{2})\\pi .\n$$\n\nSetting the derivative equal to zero and solving for $r$ gives a stationary point, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}^{′}(r) & =0 \\\\ (108-3{r}^{2})\\pi  & =0 \\\\ 108 & =3{r}^{2} \\\\ {r}^{2} & =36 \\\\ r & =6\\,\\text{cm}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that, in the last step, we only considered the positive root because the radius $r$ must be positive.\n\nFinally, we confirm that $r=6$ is a maximum using the second derivative test. The second derivative ${V}^{″}(r)$ is\n\n$$\n{V}^{″}(r)=-6\\pi r,\n$$\n\nand we can see that ${V}^{″}(6)<0.$ So, the stationary point $r=6$ is indeed a maximum of $V(r).$\n\nTherefore, the cylinder with the largest volume has a radius of $r=6\\,\\text{cm}.$" 13194139535509 17592186045417 true] [17592186101279 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535509 17592186045417 true] [17592186101279 1071 "We wish to construct a cylindrical can with a base but no lid. Our cylinder must have an exact volume of $64\\pi \\,{mm}^{3}.$ What is the smallest possible surface area of the cylinder?" 13194139535509 17592186045417 true] [17592186101279 1071 "We wish to construct a cylindrical can with a base but no lid. Our cylinder must have an exact volume of $64πmm^{3}$. What is the smallest possible surface area of the cylinder?\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139535509 17592186045417 false] [17592186101279 1074 "First, let's draw a picture of a cylinder. Let $r$ be the radius, and $h$ the height.\n\n![](images/ce/cec96bdd2335ff5635d11c4ea6016257b35bd5450778a70ecc1f5e0156ccfe9e.png)\n\nWe wish to minimize the surface area\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi rh+\\pi {r}^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe know that the volume is fixed at $64\\pi \\,{mm}^{3},$ so we also have the constraint condition\n\n$$\n64\\pi =\\pi {r}^{2}h.\n$$\n\nNow, by making $h$ the subject of the constraint equation, we have that\n\n$$\nh=\\frac{64}{{r}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nPlugging the above into our expression for $S,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(r) & =2\\pi r(\\frac{64}{{r}^{2}})+\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{128\\pi }{r}+\\pi {r}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to minimize $S,$ so we need to differentiate $S$ and set the derivative equal to zero. The derivative is \n\n$$\n{S}^{′}(r)=-\\frac{128\\pi }{{r}^{2}}+2\\pi r.\n$$\n\nSetting the derivative equal to zero and solving for $r$ gives a stationary point, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(r) & =0 \\\\ -\\frac{128\\pi }{{r}^{2}}+2\\pi r & =0 \\\\ 2\\pi r & =\\frac{128\\pi }{{r}^{2}} \\\\ 2\\pi {r}^{3} & =128\\pi  \\\\ {r}^{3} & =64 \\\\ r & =\\sqrt[3]{64} \\\\ r & =4\\,\\text{mm}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing a calculator, we can verify that if $r<4$ then ${S}^{′}(r)<0,$ and if $r>4$ then ${S}^{′}(r)>0,$ which means that the stationary point $r=4$ is indeed a minimum of $S(r).$\n\nSo the cylinder with the smallest surface area has radius $r=4\\,\\text{mm}.$\n\nFinally, to find the smallest surface area, we substitute $r=4\\,\\text{mm}$ into the equation for the surface area $S:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(4) & =\\frac{128\\pi }{4}+\\pi (4)^{2} \\\\  & =32\\pi +16\\pi  \\\\  & =48\\pi \\,{\\text{mm}}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the smallest possible surface area of the cylinder is $S=48\\pi \\,{\\text{mm}}^{2}.$" 13194139535509 17592186045417 true] [17592186101627 1061 1290 13194139535509 17592186045417 false] [17592186101627 1061 641455 13194139535509 17592186045417 true] [17592186101628 1061 1291 13194139535509 17592186045417 false] [17592186101628 1061 641456 13194139535509 17592186045417 true] [17592186101629 10