es/ed/edf5665b7cab7f51e3f252601a96d43c6f0cbfc64e5c9b8da81f0ef09d7b2f5a.png)\n\nTo graph the values of $x$ that satisfy either inequality, we overlay the two graphs on the same number line:\n\n![](images/5c/5c60839a626f2ba8a0fe4a24f4011af0fa52c00d79fbf80039bcfdbdf0a612c7.png)\n\nThus, the final solution is $x < 1$ or $x \\geq 4.$ We can write this using interval notation as\n\n$$\n(-\\infty, 1) \\cup [4,\\infty).\n$$" 13194139535509 17592186045417 true] [17592186122414 1075 #edb/ref 17592186122416 13194139535509 17592186045417 false] [17592186122414 1075 #edb/ref 17592186122419 13194139535509 17592186045417 false] [17592186122414 1075 #edb/ref 17592186509745 13194139535509 17592186045417 true] [17592186122414 1075 #edb/ref 17592186510220 13194139535509 17592186045417 true] [17592186122414 1078 #edb/ref 17592186122417 13194139535509 17592186045417 true] [17592186122468 1075 #edb/ref 17592186122469 13194139535509 17592186045417 false] [17592186122468 1075 #edb/ref 17592186122470 13194139535509 17592186045417 false] [17592186122468 1075 #edb/ref 17592186122471 13194139535509 17592186045417 false] [17592186122468 1075 #edb/ref 17592186122472 13194139535509 17592186045417 false] [17592186122468 1075 #edb/ref 17592186122473 13194139535509 17592186045417 false] [17592186122468 1075 #edb/ref 17592186509724 13194139535509 17592186045417 true] [17592186122468 1075 #edb/ref 17592186509819 13194139535509 17592186045417 true] [17592186122468 1075 #edb/ref 17592186509971 13194139535509 17592186045417 true] [17592186122468 1075 #edb/ref 17592186509997 13194139535509 17592186045417 true] [17592186122468 1075 #edb/ref 17592186510126 13194139535509 17592186045417 true] [17592186122468 1078 #edb/ref 17592186510126 13194139535509 17592186045417 true] [17592186124464 1038 #edb/ref 17592186510365 13194139535509 17592186045417 true] [17592186124464 1038 #edb/ref 17592186510409 13194139535509 17592186045417 true] [17592186124731 1050 1260.0 13194139535509 17592186045417 true] [17592186124969 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535509 17592186045417 true] [17592186124969 1071 "Find a unit normal vector to the level curve of the function $f(x, y) = 2x^{2} + 2y^{2} - y$ that passes through the point where $(x, y) = (0, 1)$." 13194139535509 17592186045417 false] [17592186124969 1071 "Find a unit normal vector to the level curve of the function $f(x,y)=2{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}-y$ that passes through the point where $(x,y)=(0,1).$" 13194139535509 17592186045417 true] [17592186124969 1074 "A normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(x,y)=(0,1)$ can be found by computing $∇f(0,1).$ \n\nSo, we first find the gradient and evaluate it at $(x,y)=(0,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}-y)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}-y)\\,j \\\\  & =(4x+0-0)\\,i+(0+4y-1)\\,j \\\\  & =4x\\,i+(4y-1)\\,j \\\\ ∇f(0,1) & =4(0)\\,i+(4(1)-1)\\,j \\\\  & =3\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize $∇f(0,1):$\n\n$$\n\\frac{∇f(0,1)}{‖∇f(0,1)‖}=\\frac{3\\,j}{3}=j\n$$" 13194139535509 17592186045417 true] [17592186124978 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535509 17592186045417 true] [17592186124978 1071 "Find a unit normal vector to the level curve of the function $f(x, y) = 2x + 4y + 5xy$ that passes through the point where $(x, y) = (2, 1)$." 13194139535509 17592186045417 false] [17592186124978 1071 "Find a unit normal vector to the level curve of the function $f(x,y)=2x+4y+5xy$ that passes through the point where $(x,y)=(2,1).$" 13194139535509 17592186045417 true] [17592186124978 1074 "A normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point where $(x,y)=(2,1)$, can be found by computing $∇f(2,1).$ \n\nSo, we first find the gradient of $f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(x,y)=(2,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x+4y+5xy)\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x+4y+5xy)\\text{ }j \\\\  & =(5y+2)\\text{ }i+(5x+4)\\text{ }j \\\\ ∇f(2,1) & =(5\\cdot 1+2)\\text{ }i+(5\\cdot 2+4)\\text{ }j \\\\  & =7i+14j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize $∇f(2,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∇f(2,1)}{‖∇f(2,1)‖} & =\\frac{7i+14j}{\\sqrt{{7}^{2}+(14)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{7i+14j}{7\\sqrt{5}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}(i+2\\,j)\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535509 17592186045417 true] [17592186125735 1064 "Given a function $z=f(x,y)$, if both partial derivatives $f_x,$ $f_y$ exist and are continuous in a neighborhood of a point $P(x_0,y_0)$ in the domain of the function, and if $\\nabla \\! f(x_0,y_0) \\neq \\mathbf 0,$ then we have the following result:\n\n*$\\nabla \\! f(x_0,y_0)$ is a normal vector to the level curve $C$ that passes through the point $P.$ *\n\nThis property is one of the most important facts in multivariable calculus since it gives a fundamental  link between\ncalculus and geometry!\n\nLet's illustrate this property with a concrete example. Consider the paraboloid $z = f (x, y)=  x^2 + y^2$ and the level curve $\\color{red}C$ of $f(x,y)$ corresponding to the point $P(1,-1).$  It is easy to see that  $\\color{red}C$ is a circle of radius $\\sqrt{2}$ with equation $x^2+y^2 = 2.$ \n\n![](images/f2/f2461dbfcde2403e6ed7de9d6288d33a4deb52319d35b7036628c3fa8b3789dc.png)\n\nIn this particular instance, the property says that\n\n*$\\nabla \\! f(1,-1)$ is a normal vector to the level curve $\\color{red}C$ that passes through the point $P(1,-1).$ *\n\n![](images/48/481b68af522b9144e232d9c775f734cd998b31a21571cbfd983c23d0f68c3688.png)\n\nLooking at the situation in the $xy$-plane, we see the following:\n\n![](images/ce/ceb353d5164d6aa089a37e77c9469d747ef4430e363e42f9d35ee6d769d9a4e4.png)\n\nLet's verify that $\\nabla f(1,-1)$ is a normal line to the level curve $C$ at the point $P(1,-1).$\n\n- First, computing the gradient, we find that $\\nabla f = \\langle 2x, 2y \\rangle.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\nabla f(1,-1) = \\langle 2, -2 \\rangle.\n$$\n\n- Then, using elementary calculus, we can show that the equation of the tangent to $C$ at $P$ is $y=x-2.$ Since this line has slope $m=1,$ it follows that the vector $\\langle 1,1 \\rangle$  is parallel to the level curve.\n\n- Finally, taking the dot product between the gradient and the tangent vector, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(1,-1)\\cdot  \\langle 1,1 \\rangle = \\langle 2, -2\\rangle \\cdot \\langle 1,1 \\rangle = 0.\n$$\n\nThis means that $\\nabla f(1,-1)$ is *perpendicular* to the tangent line to $C$ at $(1,-1).$" 13194139535509 17592186045417 true] [17592186125735 1064 "Given a function $z=f(x,y)$, if both partial derivatives $f_x,$ $f_y$ exist and are continuous in a neighborhood of a point $P(x_0,y_0)$ in the domain of the function, and if $\\nabla \\! f(x_0,y_0) \\neq \\mathbf 0,$ then we have the following result:\n\n*$\\nabla \\! f(x_0,y_0)$ is a normal vector to the level curve $C$ that passes through the point $P.$*\n\nThis property is one of the most important facts in multivariable calculus since it gives a fundamental link between calculus and geometry!\n\nLet's illustrate this property with a concrete example. Consider the paraboloid $z = f (x, y)= x^2 + y^2$ and the level curve $\\color{red}C$ of $f(x,y)$ corresponding to the point $P(1,-1).$ It is easy to see that $\\color{red}C$ is a circle of radius $\\sqrt{2}$ with equation $x^2+y^2 = 2.$\n\n![](images/f2/f2461dbfcde2403e6ed7de9d6288d33a4deb52319d35b7036628c3fa8b3789dc.png)\n\nIn this particular instance, the property says that\n\n*$\\nabla \\! f(1,-1)$ is a normal vector to the level curve $\\color{red}C$ that passes through the point $P(1,-1).$*\n\n![](images/48/481b68af522b9144e232d9c775f734cd998b31a21571cbfd983c23d0f68c3688.png)\n\nLooking at the situation in the $xy$-plane, we see the following:\n\n![](images/ce/ceb353d5164d6aa089a37e77c9469d747ef4430e363e42f9d35ee6d769d9a4e4.png)\n\nLet's verify that $\\nabla f(1,-1)$ is a normal line to the level curve $C$ at the point $P(1,-1).$\n\n- First, computing the gradient, we find that $\\nabla f = \\langle 2x, 2y \\rangle.$ Therefore, $$\\nabla f(1,-1) = \\langle 2, -2 \\rangle.$$\n- Then, using elementary calculus, we can show that the equation of the tangent to $C$ at $P$ is $y=x-2.$ Since this line has slope $m=1,$ it follows that the vector $\\langle 1,1 \\rangle$ is parallel to the level curve.\n- Finally, taking the dot product between the gradient and the tangent vector, we obtain $$\\nabla f(1,-1)\\cdot \\langle 1,1 \\rangle = \\langle 2, -2\\rangle \\cdot \\langle 1,1 \\rangle = 0.$$\n\nThis means that $\\nabla f(1,-1)$ is *perpendicular* to the tangent line to $C$ at $(1,-1).$" 13194139535509 17592186045417 false] [17592186125740 1064 "The same property holds true in the case of three or more variables. In general, if $f$ is a differentiable real-valued function of $n$ variables, $\\mathbf y$ is a point in the domain of $f,$ and if  the  gradient vector is nonzero, i.e., \n\n$$\n\\nabla f (\\mathbf y)= \\left \\langle f_{x_1}(\\mathbf y), f_{x_2}(\\mathbf y), \\cdots, f_{x_n}(\\mathbf y)\\right \\rangle \\neq \\mathbf 0,\n$$\n\nthen we have the following result:\n\n*$\\nabla f (\\mathbf y)$ is a normal vector to the level surface $f(x_1,x_2, \\cdots, x_n) =f(\\mathbf y)$ at $\\mathbf y.$*\n\nTo illustrate, let's consider the function  \n\n$$\nw = f (x, y, z)= \\dfrac 1 2\\left(x^2 + y^2 + z^2-1\\right).\n$$\n\n \n\nThe level surface corresponding to $w=0$ is the sphere $\\mathcal S$ with equation $x^2 + y^2 +z^2 =1.$ \n\nThe point $P(0,0,1)$ lies on $\\mathcal S.$ Computing $\\nabla f(0,0,1),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\\nabla f(x,y,z) &= \\langle f_x, \\: f_y, \\:f_z\\rangle \\[5pt]\n&= \\dfrac12\\langle2x, \\: 2y, \\: 2z\\rangle\\[5pt]\n&= \\langle x, \\: y, \\: z\\rangle\\[10pt]\n\n\\nabla f(0,0,1) &= \\langle 0,\\: 0,\\: 1 \\rangle.\n}\n$$\n\nNow clearly, $\\nabla f (0,0, 1)$ is perpendicular to $\\mathcal S$ at the point $(0,0,1).$\n\n![](images/88/88bf25de31ae3318379865cdf2590c15216cb984cbf3948f975caa47317be664.png)" 13194139535509 17592186045417 true] [17592186125740 1064 "The same property holds true in the case of three or more variables. In general, if $f$ is a differentiable real-valued function of $n$ variables, $\\mathbf y$ is a point in the domain of $f,$ and if the gradient vector is nonzero, i.e.,\n\n$$\\nabla f (\\mathbf y)= \\left \\langle f_{x_1}(\\mathbf y), f_{x_2}(\\mathbf y), \\cdots, f_{x_n}(\\mathbf y)\\right \\rangle \\neq \\mathbf 0,$$\n\nthen we have the following result:\n\n*$\\nabla f (\\mathbf y)$ is a normal vector to the level surface $f(x_1,x_2, \\cdots, x_n) =f(\\mathbf y)$ at $\\mathbf y.$*\n\nTo illustrate, let's consider the function\n\n$$w = f (x, y, z)= \\dfrac 1 2\\left(x^2 + y^2 + z^2-1\\right).$$\n\nThe level surface corresponding to $w=0$ is the sphere $\\mathcal S$ with equation $x^2 + y^2 +z^2 =1.$\n\nThe point $P(0,0,1)$ lies on $\\mathcal S.$ Computing $\\nabla f(0,0,1),$ we get\n\n$$\\eqalign{ \\nabla f(x,y,z) &= \\langle f_x, \\: f_y, \\:f_z\\rangle \\[5pt] &= \\dfrac12\\langle2x, \\: 2y, \\: 2z\\rangle\\[5pt] &= \\langle x, \\: y, \\: z\\rangle\\[10pt] \\nabla f(0,0,1) &= \\langle 0,\\: 0,\\: 1 \\rangle. }$$\n\nNow clearly, $\\nabla f (0,0, 1)$ is perpendicular to $\\mathcal S$ at the point $(0,0,1).$\n\n![](images/88/88bf25de31ae3318379865cdf2590c15216cb984cbf3948f975caa47317be664.png)" 13194139535509 17592186045417 false] [17592186129271 1038 #edb/ref 17592186510378 13194139535509 17592186045417 true] [17592186129271 1038 #edb/ref 17592186510461 13194139535509 17592186045417 true] [17592186129624 1068 #edb/ref 17592186127225 13194139535509 17592186045417 false] [17592186129624 1074 "The Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the surface $z=f(x,y)$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ on the surface can be written as\n\n$$\nz-{z}_{0}=(x-{x}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0})+(y-{y}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),\n$$\n\nwhere $⟨\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),-1⟩$ represents a normal vector to the tangent plane.\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3xy-\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) \\\\  & =3y \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3xy-\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) \\\\  & =3x-\\frac{1}{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the normal vector to the tangent plane is \n\n$$\n⟨3y,\\,3x-\\frac{1}{y},\\,-1⟩\n$$\n\nOur tangent plane should be perpendicular to $⟨3,5,-1⟩.$ So, the normal vector of that plane must be parallel to that vector. In other words, for some constant $c$, we must have\n\n$$\nc\\cdot ⟨3y,\\,3x-\\frac{1}{y},\\,-1⟩=⟨3,5,-1⟩.\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding coordinates, we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}3cy=3 \\\\ 3cx-\\frac{c}{y}=5 \\\\ -c=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get $c=1.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n3(1)y=3\\,⟹\\,y=1.\n$$\n\nAnd substituting $c=1$ and $y=1$ into the second equation, we have \n\n$$\n3(1)x-\\frac{1}{1}=5\\,⟹\\,x=2.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $x=2$ and $y=1$ into our function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =3xy-\\operatorname{ln}⁡y \\\\  & =3(2)(1)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1) \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent plane is perpendicular to the vector  $v$ at $(2,1,6).$" 13194139535509 17592186045417 true] [17592186129624 1074 "The Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the surface $z=f(x,y)$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ on the surface can be written as\n\n$$\nz-{z}_{0}=(x-{x}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0})+(y-{y}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),\n$$\n\nwhere $⟨\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),-1⟩$ represents a normal vector to the tangent plane.\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3xy-\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) \\\\  & =3y \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3xy-\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) \\\\  & =3x-\\frac{1}{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the normal vector to the tangent plane is \n\n$$\n⟨3y,\\,3x-\\frac{1}{y},\\,-1⟩\n$$\n\nOur tangent plane should be perpendicular to $⟨3,5,-1⟩.$ So, the normal vector of that plane must be parallel to that vector. In other words, for some constant $c$, we must have\n\n$$\nc\\cdot ⟨3y,\\,3x-\\frac{1}{y},\\,-1⟩=⟨3,5,-1⟩.\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding coordinates, we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}3cy=3 \\\\ 3cx-\\frac{c}{y}=5 \\\\ -c=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get $c=1.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n3(1)y=3\\,⟹\\,y=1.\n$$\n\nAnd substituting $c=1$ and $y=1$ into the second equation, we have \n\n$$\n3(1)x-\\frac{1}{1}=5\\,⟹\\,x=2.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $x=2$ and $y=1$ into our function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =3xy-\\operatorname{ln}⁡y \\\\  & =3(2)(1)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1) \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent plane is perpendicular to the vector  $v$ at $(2,1,6).$" 13194139535509 17592186045417 false] [17592186133314 1078 #edb/ref 17592186133315 13194139535509 17592186045417 true] [17592186133798 1038 #edb/ref 17592186510371 13194139535509 17592186045417 true] [17592186133798 1038 #edb/ref 17592186510452 13194139535509 17592186045417 true] [17592186133803 1038 #edb/ref 17592186510364 13194139535509 17592186045417 true] [17592186133803 1038 #edb/ref 17592186510447 13194139535509 17592186045417 true] [17592186133906 1038 #edb/ref 17592186510449 13194139535509 17592186045417 true] [17592186133906 1038 #edb/ref 17592186510454 13194139535509 17592186045417 true] [17592186134546 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535509 17592186045417 true] [17592186134546 1071 "Given that $A = QR$ is a QR factorization of a $4 \\cdot 3$ matrix $A$ with linearly independent columns, which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. The columns of $Q$ are orthogonal\n2. $Q$ is a $4 \\cdot 3$ matrix\n3. $R$ is a $4 \\cdot 3$ matrix" 13194139535509 17592186045417 false] [17592186134546 1071 "Given that $A=QR$ is a QR factorization of a $4\\times 3$ matrix $A$ with linearly i