ng $v$ by its norm, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n|{v}_{11}|+|{v}_{21}|=|-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}|+|\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}|=\\sqrt{2}.\n$$" 13194139535509 17592186045417 true] [17592186172106 1038 #edb/ref 17592186510379 13194139535509 17592186045417 true] [17592186172106 1038 #edb/ref 17592186510417 13194139535509 17592186045417 true] [17592186172129 1038 #edb/ref 17592186510397 13194139535509 17592186045417 true] [17592186172129 1038 #edb/ref 17592186510398 13194139535509 17592186045417 true] [17592186172129 1038 #edb/ref 17592186510430 13194139535509 17592186045417 true] [17592186187421 1075 #edb/ref 17592186510025 13194139535509 17592186045417 true] [17592186187421 1078 #edb/ref 17592186510025 13194139535509 17592186045417 true] [17592186188179 1071 "What is $0.2 \\,\\textrm{kg}$ expressed in $\\textrm g$?" 13194139535509 17592186045417 false] [17592186188179 1071 "What is $0.2 \\,\\textrm{kg}$ expressed in $\\textrm g?$" 13194139535509 17592186045417 true] [17592186188179 1074 "We start by recalling the conversion table for mass units.\n\n| Name | Symbol | Conversion |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| Kilogram | $\\textrm{kg}$ | $1\\,\\textrm{kg} = 1\\,000\\,\\textrm{g}$ |\n| Gram | $\\textrm{g}$ | Base Unit |\n| Milligram | $\\textrm{mg}$ | $1\\,000\\,\\textrm{mg} = 1\\,\\textrm{g}$ |\n\nThere are $1000\\,\\textrm g$ per $\\textrm{kg}$, which we can write as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1000\\,\\textrm g}{\\textrm{kg}}\n$$\n\nThis is our conversion factor.\n\nNow, multiplying $0.2\\,\\textrm{kg}$ by our conversion factor, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n0.2kg &= 0.2kg \\cdot \\frac{1000g}{kg} \\\\\n&= 0.2kg \\cdot \\frac{1000g}{kg} \\\\\n&= 0.2 \\cdot 1000g \\\\\n&= 200g\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $0.2\\,\\textrm{kg}$ is equivalent to $200 \\,\\text{g}$." 13194139535509 17592186045417 false] [175921861881