converting between different units of measurement.\n\nFor example, let's use units to convert $0.03\\,\\textrm{kg}$ into grams. We start by recalling the conversion table for mass units.\n\n| Name | Symbol | Conversion |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| Kilogram | $\\textrm{kg}$ | $1\\,\\textrm{kg} = 1\\,000\\,\\textrm{g}$ |\n| Gram | $\\textrm{g}$ | Base Unit |\n| Milligram | $\\textrm{mg}$ | $1\\,000\\,\\textrm{mg} = 1\\,\\textrm{g}$ |\n\nSince we're converting from kilograms to grams, we need a conversion between these two units. According to our table, there are $1000\\,\\textrm g$ per $\\textrm{kg}$. We can write this ratio as a fraction, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1000\\,\\textrm g}{\\textrm{kg}}\n$$\n\nThis is our **conversion factor.** It's important to write the conversion factor with the units included.\n\nTo convert $0.03 \\,\\textrm{kg}$ to grams, we multiply this quantity by our conversion factor as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n0.03kg &= 0.03kg \\cdot \\frac{1000g}{kg}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the kilogram units $(\\textrm{kg})$ cancel, leaving only gram units $(\\textrm{g})$. Consequently, our expression can be simplified as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n0.03kg &= 0.03kg \\cdot \\frac{1000g}{kg} \\\\\n&= 0.03kg \\cdot \\frac{1000g}{kg} \\\\\n&= 0.03 \\cdot 1000g \\\\\n&= 30g\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $0.03 \\,\\textrm{kg}$ is equivalent to $30 \\,\\text{g}$.\n\nNote the following:\n\n- When multiplying by the conversion factor, the units we want to remove should *always* cancel, and the units we wish to keep should *always* remain. If this isn't the case, we've made a mistake somewhere.\n- We can think of the conversion factor as equivalent to the number $1$, and multiplying a quantity by $1$ doesn't change it. Therefore, multiplying $0.03\\,\\textrm{kg}$ by the conversion factor doesn't change the *size* of the quantity, though it does change the units.\n\nFinally, notice that, in this example, we've converted from a \"larger\" unit of mass $(\\textrm{kg})$ to a smaller one $(\\textrm{g})$. We'll see how to use units to convert from smaller units of mass to larger units later in this lesson." 13194139535509 17592186045417 false] [17592186189493 1064 "We can manipulate units the same way as variables, which often provides a convenient way to solve problems. One type of problem where units can help is converting between different units of measurement. \n\nFor example, let's use units to convert $0.03\\,\\textrm{kg}$ into grams. We start by recalling the conversion table for mass units. \n\n| Name | Symbol | Conversion |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Kilogram | $\\textrm{kg}$ | $1\\,\\textrm{kg} = 1\\,000\\,\\textrm{g}$ |\n| Gram | $\\textrm{g}$ | Base Unit |\n| Milligram | $\\textrm{mg}$ | $1\\,000\\,\\textrm{mg} = 1\\,\\textrm{g}$ |\n\nSince we're converting from kilograms to grams, we need a conversion between these two units. According to our table, there are $1000\\,\\textrm g$ per $\\textrm{kg}.$ We can write this ratio as a fraction, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1000\\,\\textrm g}{\\textrm{kg}}\n$$\n\nThis is our **conversion factor.** It's important to write the conversion factor with the units included.\n\nTo convert $0.03 \\,\\textrm{kg}$ to grams, we multiply this quantity by our conversion factor as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.03\\,\\text{kg} & =0.03\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot \\frac{1\\,000\\,\\text{g}}{\\text{kg}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the kilogram units $(\\textrm{kg})$ cancel, leaving only gram units $(\\textrm{g}).$ Consequently, our expression can be simplified as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.03\\,\\text{kg} & =0.03\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot \\frac{1\\,000\\,\\text{g}}{\\text{kg}} \\\\  & =0.03\\,\\cancel{\\text{kg}}\\cdot \\frac{1\\,000\\,\\text{g}}{\\cancel{\\text{kg}}} \\\\  & =0.03\\cdot 1\\,000\\,\\text{g} \\\\  & =30\\,\\text{g}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $0.03 \\,\\textrm{kg}$ is equivalent to $30 \\,\\text{g}.$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- When multiplying by the conversion factor, the units we want to remove should *always* cancel, and the units we wish to keep should *always* remain. If this isn't the case, we've made a mistake somewhere.\n\n- We can think of the conversion factor as equivalent to the number $1,$ and multiplying a quantity by $1$ doesn't change it. Therefore, multiplying $0.03\\,\\textrm{kg}$ by the conversion factor doesn't change the *size* of the quantity, though it does change the units.\n\nFinally, notice that, in this example, we've converted from a \"larger\" unit of mass $(\\textrm{kg})$ to a smaller one $(\\textrm{g}).$ We'll see how to use units to convert from smaller units of mass to larger units later in this lesson." 13194139535509 17592186045417 true] [17592186189495 1064 "We've seen how to use units to convert from larger units of mass to smaller ones. Let's now learn how to convert from smaller units to larger ones.\n\nFor example, suppose we want to convert $600\\,\\textrm{mg}$ to grams. We start by recalling the conversion table for mass units.\n\n| Name | Symbol | Conversion |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| Kilogram | $\\textrm{kg}$ | $1\\,\\textrm{kg} = 1\\,000\\,\\textrm{g}$ |\n| Gram | $\\textrm{g}$ | Base Unit |\n| Milligram | $\\textrm{mg}$ | $1\\,000\\,\\textrm{mg} = 1\\,\\textrm{g}$ |\n\nThere are $1000\\,\\textrm {mg}$ per $\\textrm{g}$, which we can write as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1000\\,\\textrm{mg}}{\\textrm{g}}\n$$\n\nHowever, since we're converting from a smaller unit of mass $(\\textrm{mg})$ to a larger one $(\\textrm{g})$, we construct our conversion factor by taking the *reciprocal* of the above:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{g}}{1000\\,\\textrm{mg}}\n$$\n\nNow, multiplying $600 \\,\\textrm{mg}$ by our conversion factor, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n600mg &= 600mg \\cdot \\frac{g}{1000mg} \\\\\n&= 600mg \\cdot \\frac{g}{1000mg} \\\\\n&= 600 \\cdot \\frac{g}{1000} \\\\\n&= \\frac{600}{1000}g \\\\\n&= 0.6g\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $600 \\,\\textrm{mg}$ is equivalent to $0.6\\,\\text{g}$.\n\nNote the following:\n\n- When we say that grams are a \"larger unit of mass\" than milligrams, this simply means that $1\\,\\textrm g$ is a larger mass than $1\\,\\textrm{mg}$.\n- If you forget to take the reciprocal to find the conversion factor, the units will not cancel when you multiply. This indicates that a mistake has occurred. In such cases, it's best to double-check that you're using the correct conversion factor." 13194139535509 17592186045417 false] [17592186189495 1064 "We've seen how to use units to convert from larger units of mass to smaller ones. Let's now learn how to convert from smaller units to larger ones.\n\nFor example, suppose we want to convert $600\\,\\textrm{mg}$ to grams. We start by recalling the conversion table for mass units. \n\n| Name | Symbol | Conversion |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Kilogram | $\\textrm{kg}$ | $1\\,\\textrm{kg} = 1\\,000\\,\\textrm{g}$ |\n| Gram | $\\textrm{g}$ | Base Unit |\n| Milligram | $\\textrm{mg}$ | $1\\,000\\,\\textrm{mg} = 1\\,\\textrm{g}$ |\n\nThere are $1000\\,\\textrm {mg}$ per $\\textrm{g},$ which we can write as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1000\\,\\textrm{mg}}{\\textrm{g}}\n$$\n\nHowever, since we're converting from a smaller unit of mass $(\\textrm{mg})$ to a larger one $(\\textrm{g}),$ we construct our conversion factor by taking the *reciprocal* of the above:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{g}}{1000\\,\\textrm{mg}}\n$$\n\nNow, multiplying $600 \\,\\textrm{mg}$ by our conversion factor, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}600\\,\\text{mg} & =600\\,\\text{mg}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{g}}{1\\,000\\,\\text{mg}} \\\\  & =600\\,\\cancel{\\text{mg}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{g}}{1\\,000\\,\\cancel{\\text{mg}}} \\\\  & =600\\cdot \\frac{\\text{g}}{1\\,000} \\\\  & =\\frac{600}{1000}\\,\\text{g} \\\\  & =0.6\\,\\text{g}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $600 \\,\\textrm{mg}$ is equivalent to $0.6\\,\\text{g}.$\n\nNote the following: \n\n- When we say that grams are a \"larger unit of mass\" than milligrams, this simply means that $1\\,\\textrm g$ is a larger mass than $1\\,\\textrm{mg}.$\n\n- If you forget to take the reciprocal to find the conversion factor, the units will not cancel when you multiply. This indicates that a mistake has occurred. In such cases, it's best to double-check that you're using the correct conversion factor." 13194139535509 17592186045417 true] [17592186190048 1078 #edb/ref 17592186190053 13194139535509 17592186045417 true] [17592186192684 1038 #edb/ref 17592186510380 13194139535509 17592186045417 true] [17592186192684 1038 #edb/ref 17592186510401 13194139535509 17592186045417 true] [17592186192701 1038 #edb/ref 17592186510422 13194139535509 17592186045417 true] [17592186192702 1038 #edb/ref 17592186510395 13194139535509 17592186045417 true] [17592186192712 1038 #edb/ref 17592186510383 13194139535509 17592186045417 true] [17592186192712 1038 #edb/ref 17592186510425 13194139535509 17592186045417 true] [17592186192797 1038 #edb/ref 17592186510388 13194139535509 17592186045417 true] [17592186192797 1038 #edb/ref 17592186510408 13194139535509 17592186045417 true] [17592186192799 1038 #edb/ref 17592186510400 13194139535509 17592186045417 true] [17592186192860 1038 #edb/ref 17592186510391 13194139535509 17592186045417 true] [17592186193027 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535509 17592186045417 true] [17592186193027 1071 "Let $f(x)$ be positive and strictly increasing for $x ∈ [1, 4]$, and differentiable for $x ∈ (1, 4)$. Suppose we approximate the area under the curve $y = g(x) = x \\cdot f(x)$ over the interval $[1, 4]$ using a left Riemann sum. Given that $g(x) > 0$, which of the following statements must be true?\n\n1. $f^{′}(x) > 0$\n2. $g^{′}(x) > 0$\n3. The Riemann sum underestimates the true area." 13194139535509 17592186045417 false] [17592186193027 1071 "Let $f(x)$ be positive and strictly increasing for $x\\in [1,4],$ and differentiable for $x\\in (1,4).$ Suppose we approximate the area under the curve $y=g(x)=x\\cdot f(x)$ over the interval $[1,4]$ using a left Riemann sum. Given that $g(x)>0,$ which of the following statements must be true?\n\nI. ${f}^{′}(x)>0$\n\nII. ${g}^{′}(x)>0$\n\nIII. The Riemann sum underestimates the true area." 13194139535509 17592186045417 true] [17592186193027 1074 "We're given that $g(x)=x\\cdot f(x)>0.$ Now, recall the following:\n\n- If $g(x)$ is strictly increasing, the left Riemann sum gives an *underestimate* of the area under the curve $y=g(x).$\n\n- If $g(x)$ is strictly decreasing, the left Riemann sum gives an *overestimate* of the area under the curve $y=g(x).$\n\nLet's now consider each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. Since $f(x)$ is strictly increasing, we have ${f}^{′}(x)>0.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Differentiating $g(x)=x\\cdot f(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(x) & =(x)^{′}\\cdot f(x)+x\\cdot {f}^{′}(x) \\\\  & =f(x)+x\\cdot {f}^{′}(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x)$ is positive and strictly increasing over the interval $(1,4),$ we have \n\n$$\nx>0,\\,f(x)>0,\\,{f}^{′}(x)>0.\n$$\n\nHence, we obtain\n\n$$\n{g}^{′}(x)={{f(x)}_{⏟}}_{>0}+{{x}_{⏟}}_{>0}\\cdot {{{f}^{′}(x)}_{⏟}}_{>0}={{f(x)}_{⏟}}_{>0}+{{x\\cdot {f}^{′}(x)}_{⏟}}_{>0}>0.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since ${g}^{′}(x)>0,$ the function $g(x)$ is strictly increasing. Therefore, the left Riemann sum gives an *underestimate* of the area under the curve $y=x\\cdot f(x).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" 13194139535509 17592186045417 true] [17592186193179 1068 #edb/ref 17592186190960 13194139535509 17592186045417 false] [17592186193179 1071 "![](images/b9/b95c538613ee8672e0f1cac970b7af230ec40bf3075e609b1e03f7cad4382fb1.png)\n\nConsider the set $A$ shown above. The interior of $A$ is \n\n$$\n\\text{int}\\,A=\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,0<x<1,\\,\\bbox[5px,border:1px solid black]{\\phantom{AAAAA}}\\}.\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression?" 13194139535509 17592186045417 true] [17592186193179 1071 "![](images/b9/b95c538613ee8672e0f1cac970b7af230ec40bf3075e609b1e03f7cad4382fb1.png)\n\nConsider the set $A$ shown above. The interior of $A$ is \n\n$$\n\\text{int}\\,A={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,0<x<1,\\,}.\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression?" 13194139535509 17592186045417 false] [17592186193179 1074 "The boundary of a set $A,$ denoted $∂A,$ is the set that contains all the boundary points of $A.$\n\nThe set $A$ and its boundary $∂A$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/19/19e8e52881066864cba562ed2a8cdc2944b9dae416c7124fdee207cdad1d8bdf.png)\n\nThe interior of a set $A,$ denoted $\\text{int}\\,A,$ is the set containing all the interior points of $A.$ It contains every point in $A$ except the boundary points: \n\n$$\n\\text{int}\\,A=A-∂A\n$$\n\nThe interior of $A$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/90/907e996fb7aea36e0c924feb0f5d9dc6804c768be775953d277e84b0145ef5a0.png)\n\nSo, in this case, the interior of $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{int}\\,A=\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,0<x<1,\\,-1<y<0\\}.\n$$" 13194139535509 17592186045417 true] [17592186193179 1074 "The boundary of a set $A,$ denoted $∂A,$ is the set that contains all the boundary points of $A.$\n\nThe set $A$ and its boundary $∂A$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/19/19e8e52881066864cba562ed2a8cdc2944b9dae416c7124fdee207cdad1d8bdf.png)\n\nThe interior of a set $A,$ denoted $\\text{int}\\,A,$ is the set containing all the interior points of $A.$ It contains every point in $A$ except the boundary points: \n\n$$\n\\text{int}\\,A=A-∂A\n$$\n\nThe interior of $A$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/90/907e996fb7aea36e0c924feb0f5d9dc6804c768be775953d277e84b0145ef5a0.png)\n\nSo, in this case, the interior of $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{int}\\,A={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,0<x<1,\\,-1<y<0}.\n$$" 13194139535509 17592186045417 false] [17592186194435 1075 #edb/ref 17592186194436 13194139535509 17592186045417 false] [17592186194435 1075 #edb/ref 17592186194437 13194139535509 17592186045417 false] [17592186194435 1075 #edb/ref 17592186194438 13194139535509 17592186045417 false] [17592186194435 1075 #edb/ref 17592186194439 13194139535509 17592186045417 false] [17592186194435 1075 #edb/ref 17592186194440 13194139535509 17592186045417 false] [17592186194435 1075 #edb/ref 17592186509786 13194139535509 17592186045417 true] [17592186194435 1075 #edb/ref 17592186510031 13194139535509 17592186045417 true] [17592186194435 1075 #edb/ref 17592186510169 13194139535509 17592186045417 true] [17592186194435 1075 #edb/ref 17592186510189 13194139535509 17592186045417 true] [17592186194435 1075 #edb/ref 17592186510239 13194139535509 17592186045417 true] [17592186194435 1078 #edb/ref 17592186510031 13194139535509 17592186045417 true] [17592186194447 1078 #edb/ref 17592186194449 13194139535509 17592186045417 true] [17592186194483 1075 #edb/ref 17592186194484 13194139535509 17592186045417 false] [17592186194483 1075 #edb/ref 17592186194485 13194139535509 17592186045417 false] [17592186194483 1075 #edb/ref 17592186194486 13194139535509 17592186045417 false] [17592186194483 1075 #edb/ref 17592186194487 13194139535509 17592186045417 false] [17592186194483 1075 #edb/ref 17592186194488 13194139535509 17592186045417 false] [17592186194483 1075 #edb/ref 17592186509897 13194139535509 17592186045417 true] [17592186194483 1075 #edb/ref 17592186510139 13194139535509 17592186045417 true] [17592186194483 1075 #edb/ref 17592186510201 13194139535509 17592186045417 true] [17592186194483 1075 #edb/ref 17592186510203 13194139535509 17592186045417 true] [17592186194483 1075 #edb/ref 17592186510232 13194139535509 17592186045417 true] [17592186194483 1078 #edb/ref 17592186510201 13194139535509 17592186045417 true] [17592186198354 1038 #edb/ref 17592186510389 13194139535509 17592186045417 true] [17592186198354 1038 #edb/ref 17592186510411 13194139535509 17592186045417 true] [17592186198412 1038 #edb/ref 17592186510373 13194139535509 17592186045417 true] [17592186198412 1038 #edb/ref 17592186510464 13194139535509 17592186045417 true] [17592186198413 1038 #edb/ref 17592186510385 13194139535509 17592186045417