{{field-1}}.\n$$\n\nSince $f$ is \n{{field-2}}\nand\n{{field-3}}\non $R,$ we may differentiate its Fourier series term-by-term to get\n\n$$\n-\\frac{3}{2}x∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{{field-4}}\\cdot {{field-5}}.\n$$" 13194139535509 17592186045417 false] [17592186317374 1071 "Let $f(x)$ be the $6$-periodic extension of the function $F(x)=6{x}^{2}-2$ defined on $[-3,3).$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below, showing the derivation of the Fourier series of the $6$-periodic extension of $G(x)=12x$ from the corresponding Fourier series of $f(x).$\n\nThe Fourier series of $f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\n6{x}^{2}-2∼16+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{216(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{{field-1}}.\n$$\n\nSince $f$ is \n{{field-2}}\nand\n{{field-3}}\non $R,$ we may differentiate its Fourier series term-by-term to get\n\n$$\n12x∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{{field-4}}\\cdot {{field-5}}.\n$$" 13194139535509 17592186045417 true] [17592186317374 1071 "Let $f(x)$ be the $6$-periodic extension of the function $F(x)=6{x}^{2}-2$ defined on $[-3,3).$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below, showing the derivation of the Fourier series of the $6$-periodic extension of $G(x)=12x$ from the corresponding Fourier series of $f(x).$The Fourier series of $f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\n6{x}^{2}-2∼16+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{216(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{{field-1}}.\n$$\n\nSince $f$ is \n{{field-2}}\nand\n{{field-3}}\non $R,$ we may differentiate its Fourier series term-by-term to get\n\n$$\n12x∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{{field-4}}\\cdot {{field-5}}.\n$$" 13194139535509 17592186045417 false] [17592186342100 1074 "The Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the surface $z=f(x,y)$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ on the surface can be written as\n\n$$\nz-{z}_{0}=(x-{x}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0})+(y-{y}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),\n$$\n\nwhere $⟨\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),-1⟩$ represents a normal vector to the tangent plane.\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y+{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡y \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y+{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{y}+2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the normal vector to the tangent plane is \n\n$$\n⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡y,\\,\\frac{x}{y}+2y,\\,-1⟩\n$$\n\nOur tangent plane should be perpendicular to $⟨0,2,-2⟩.$ So, the normal vector of that plane must be parallel to that vector. In other words, for some constant $c$, we must have\n\n$$\nc\\cdot ⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡y,\\,\\frac{x}{y}+2y,\\,-1⟩=⟨0,2,-2⟩.\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding coordinates, we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}c\\operatorname{ln}⁡y=0 \\\\ \\frac{cx}{y}+2cy=2 \\\\ -c=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get $c=2.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{ln}⁡y=0\\,⟹\\,y=1.\n$$\n\nAnd substituting $c=2$ and $y=1$ into the second equation, we have \n\n$$\n\\frac{2x}{1}+2(2)(1)=2\\,⟹\\,x=-1.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $x=-1$ and $y=1$ into our function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =(-1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡1+{1}^{2} \\\\  & =0+1 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent plane is perpendicular to the vector $v$ at $(-1,1,1).$" 13194139535509 17592186045417 true] [17592186342100 1074 "The Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the surface $z=f(x,y)$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ on the surface can be written as\n\n$$\nz-{z}_{0}=(x-{x}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0})+(y-{y}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),\n$$\n\nwhere $⟨\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),-1⟩$ represents a normal vector to the tangent plane.\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y+{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡y \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y+{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{y}+2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the normal vector to the tangent plane is \n\n$$\n⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡y,\\,\\frac{x}{y}+2y,\\,-1⟩\n$$\n\nOur tangent plane should be perpendicular to $⟨0,2,-2⟩.$ So, the normal vector of that plane must be parallel to that vector. In other words, for some constant $c$, we must have\n\n$$\nc\\cdot ⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡y,\\,\\frac{x}{y}+2y,\\,-1⟩=⟨0,2,-2⟩.\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding coordinates, we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}c\\operatorname{ln}⁡y=0 \\\\ \\frac{cx}{y}+2cy=2 \\\\ -c=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get $c=2.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{ln}⁡y=0\\,⟹\\,y=1.\n$$\n\nAnd substituting $c=2$ and $y=1$ into the second equation, we have \n\n$$\n\\frac{2x}{1}+2(2)(1)=2\\,⟹\\,x=-1.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $x=-1$ and $y=1$ into our function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =(-1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡1+{1}^{2} \\\\  & =0+1 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent plane is perpendicular to the vector $v$ at $(-1,1,1).$" 13194139535509 17592186045417 false] [17592186344834 1078 #edb/ref 17592186343227 13194139535509 17592186045417 true] [17592186344834 1078 #edb/ref 17592186343461 13194139535509 17592186045417 false] [17592186378843 1071 "![](images/80/80bde6a20ceed7c052a87eda6f05c542f6046de7014f6402f5f453b6610f8d42.png)\n\nConsider the set $A,$ shown above. The boundary of $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n∂A=\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,\\bbox[5px,border:1px solid black]{\\phantom{AAAAA}}\\,\\}.\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression?" 13194139535509 17592186045417 true] [17592186378843 1071 "![](images/80/80bde6a20ceed7c052a87eda6f05c542f6046de7014f6402f5f453b6610f8d42.png)\n\nConsider the set $A,$ shown above. The boundary of $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n∂A={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,\\,}.\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression?" 13194139535509 17592186045417 false] [17592186378843 1074 "The boundary of a se